{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PLSSPmo4zLH2evoNAxsgP4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017Ve6VeYsmSXUuKEKZ2Ge95","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for EMNLP 2026 ARR. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. \n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR scoring (Overall Assessment, half-point increments):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference acceptance\n- 3 = Findings acceptance\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5).\n\n## Paper summary\n\"Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis\"\n\nThe paper applies Mantel-Haenszel DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items), finding 31.3% of items show large temporal DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts — 142× above permutation null (z=622). It makes 4 contributions:\n1. Temporal instability characterization (31.3% C-rate, robust to 6 confounds)\n2. Ground truth meta-critique (web-overlap labels are mismatched for DIF; enrichment≈1.0, χ²=1.28 p=0.258)\n3. Semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000, portable to GSM8K with ΔTPR=+0.818)\n4. DIF as diagnostic lens (ρ≈0.997 ranking stability but z=8.5 cohort bias upon DIF removal)\n\nReplicates across 6 benchmarks (17.9-31.3%). Confound elimination covers: architecture (99.98% decoder-only), ability gap (d=-0.17, 2.4pp confound), local independence (Q3=0.009), model non-independence (family-cap C%≤random at every K), IRT θ-conditioning (r=0.9986), non-uniform DIF (24.8% additional).\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### Methodology\n1. Is MH-DIF correctly applied to LLM benchmarks? Any violations of MH assumptions (e.g., treating models as \"examinees\", binary response matrix validity)?\n2. Is the semi-synthetic validation framework (§3.2) sound? Does incorrect→correct flipping adequately model real contamination?\n3. Is Proposition 1 (cross-subject matching → ΔFPR=0) correctly stated and proven? Is the \"proof\" rigorous or just empirical?\n4. Are the 5 CTT total-score strata sufficient for MH conditioning, or could finer stratification change results?\n\n### Experiments\n5. Is the confound elimination (§4.3) thorough? Any missing confounds?\n6. Is the permutation test (§4.2) properly constructed? 1000 permutations sufficient?\n7. Simpson's paradox analysis (Table 4): Are the logistic regression models properly specified? Is R²=0.0007 the right metric?\n8. Cross-benchmark replication (Table 5): Are the 6 benchmarks independent enough? Do they share models?\n9. GSM1K correlation (r=-0.478, N=33): Is this appropriately hedged?\n10. DIF-cleaned demonstration (§4.7): Is z=8.5 cohort asymmetry convincing?\n11. Any statistical issues (multiple comparisons, selective reporting, p-hacking risk)?\n12. Missing experiments that would strengthen or weaken claims?\n\n### Experiment coverage\nThe experiment plan included these experiments. Check which are adequately covered vs missing:\n- Temporal DIF on MMLU (plan_001) — should be main result\n- RL robustness (plan_002) — N=13, directional only\n- ConStat/DVD eval (plan_003a/b) — not executed, cited from literature\n- Cross-benchmark GSM8K (plan_004) — should include semi-synthetic + GSM1K correlation\n- Bifactor IRT (plan_005) — killed, mentioned in Limitations\n- Cumulative Bayesian DIF (plan_007) — simulation in appendix\n- Graceful degradation (plan_008) — in §4.7/appendix\n- Item-Focused Trees (plan_009) — appendix\n- Architecture stratified (plan_010) — §4.3\n- Semi-synthetic (plan_011) — §4.6\n- Multi-benchmark (plan_028) — Table 5\n- Permutation null (plan_029) — Table 3\n- Simpson's paradox (plan_030) — Table 4\n- Temporal granularity (plan_031) — §4.4\n- DIF-cleaned demo (plan_032) — §4.7\n- Subject concentration (plan_033) — §4.1\n\n### Reproducibility\n13. Is there enough detail to reproduce (data source, code, parameters)?\n14. METABENCH is public, but are the exact cohort definitions reproducible?\n\n### Limitations assessment\n15. Are the 3 stated limitations (1D IRT misspecification, simplified injection, RL underpowered) the right ones? Missing limitations?\n\n## Output format\nStructure your review as:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, be specific with section/table references]\n\n### Weaknesses  \n[Numbered list, be specific. Classify each as Major or Minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Experiments Assessment\n[Which planned experiments are adequately covered, which are missing, and does it matter?]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5  \n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (with justification referencing the EMNLP scale)\n\n### Confidence\n[1-5 scale, with justification]\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Ground every criticism in specific evidence from the paper."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":46355,"cache_read_input_tokens":24148,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":46355},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e51e0195-9c84-4e56-b272-a71ad93d54d2","uuid":"1fda0924-da78-4086-b4b1-c0a3d85b2450"}
