{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ho2VN6Hjd519MfFVbrBevj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\\label{sec:related_work}\n3\t\n4\t\\paragraph{Contamination Detection.}\n5\tA growing family of methods detects benchmark contamination through model behavior or training data analysis. Membership inference approaches estimate whether a model has seen specific items during training: Min-K\\% \\citep{shi2024mink} and Min-K\\%++ \\citep{zhang2024minkpp} use token-level log-likelihood statistics, while Time Travel \\citep{golchin2024timetravel} probes models with temporal cues. Performance-based detectors identify contamination through statistical anomalies: ConStat \\citep{dekoninck2024constat} tests performance gaps between original and perturbed items, DVD \\citep{liang2026dvd} uses distributional verification, and Self-Critique \\citep{tao2026selfcritique} employs model self-evaluation. \\citet{balloccu2024leak} provide a broader taxonomy of leakage detection strategies. These methods share a common validation practice: they verify detection accuracy against web-overlap or n-gram labels \\citep{li2024contamination} that classify items based on verbatim matches in publicly accessible corpora. Such labels measure \\emph{global exposure} (whether an item exists in training data) rather than \\emph{differential item functioning} (whether an item behaves differently across model cohorts). This global-vs-differential distinction motivates our ground truth analysis (Claim~2, \\Cref{sec:groundtruth}). Work on LLM memorization \\citep{carlini2022quantifying, tirumala2022memorization} establishes that memorization depends on data frequency and model scale, reinforcing the distinction between global exposure and differential behavior. \\citet{wang2026fragility} show that existing contamination detectors are fragile under reinforcement learning, further motivating behavioral approaches grounded in psychometric theory.\n6\t\n7\t\\paragraph{IRT in LLM Evaluation.}\n8\tIRT adoption in LLM evaluation has accelerated: \\citet{lalor2019irt} introduced IRT to NLP, \\citet{vania2021irt} assessed test set discriminability, and \\citet{rodriguez2021evaluation} demonstrated that not all evaluation examples are equally informative. Recent extensions include PSN-IRT \\citep{zhou2026psnirt} for benchmark quality at scale, TinyBenchmarks \\citep{maiapolo2024tinybenchmarks} for IRT-based item selection, and scalability coefficients for detecting bad benchmark items \\citep{hardy2026scalability}. The closest concurrent work is Growing Pains \\citep{habba2026growingpains}, which addresses score comparability through fixed-parameter calibration \\citep[FPC;][]{kim2019fpc}. Our work is complementary: Growing Pains assumes anchor item stability, while DIF analysis diagnoses which items violate this assumption.\n9\t\n10\t\\paragraph{DIF Methodology and Measurement Invariance.}\n11\tThe Mantel-Haenszel procedure for DIF detection was introduced by \\citet{holland1988mh} and remains the most widely used method in educational testing due to its simplicity and well-characterized statistical properties \\citep{clauser1998dif}. Alternative approaches include logistic regression DIF \\citep{swaminathan1990logistic}, SIBTEST \\citep{shealy1993sibtest}, Lord's $\\chi^2$ test \\citep{lord1980irt}, and the Raju area method \\citep{raju1988area}; \\citet{zumbo1999handbook} provides a comprehensive comparison. DIF analysis is a specific instance of measurement invariance testing \\citep{meredith1993invariance, vandenberg2000invariance}, which examines whether a measurement instrument functions equivalently across populations. MIMIC models offer a latent-variable approach to the same problem \\citep{muthen1985mimic, woods2009mimic}, and \\citet{teresi2007dif} reviews DIF methods across applied domains. Cross-subject matching---computing the stratification variable from domains other than the one being tested---has psychometric precedent in the DIF literature \\citep{nandakumar1993dif,donoghue1993mh}; our adaptation addresses LLM-specific challenges of multi-domain local dependence and simultaneous contamination control at scale. We do not modify the MH procedure; our contribution is the application context and the empirical findings it reveals. The scale of this analysis (5,227 examinees across a 57-subject multidimensional ability space) exceeds typical educational testing applications, where \\citet{belzak2020smallsample} note that DIF methods can behave differently at extreme sample sizes. The primary methodological challenge is the impact-versus-DIF confound: genuine ability differences between temporal cohorts can inflate DIF estimates. We address this through matched-ability quintile analysis, IRT $\\theta$-conditioning, and a random-split control (\\Cref{sec:temporal}).\n12\t\n13\t\\paragraph{Benchmark Quality and Design.}\n14\tThe Benchmark Health Index~\\citep{liu2026bhi} assesses static benchmark quality; our temporal DIF analysis is complementary, diagnosing \\emph{when} measurement properties shift rather than evaluating them at a single snapshot. \\citet{maeda2025difllm} predict which item features associate with DIF in educational settings; we instead detect DIF empirically across LLM cohorts at scale. Dynamic benchmarks \\citep{kiela2021dynabench, li2024latesteval, white2025livebench} mitigate contamination by construction, yet none provides psychometric equating across versions. Chatbot Arena \\citep{chiang2024chatbot} sidesteps static benchmarks through pairwise preference. Broader surveys \\citep{chang2024survey, burnell2023rethink} identify benchmark saturation and lack of item-level transparency as systemic challenges that our DIF analysis directly addresses.\n15\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017Ve6VeYsmSXUuKEKZ2Ge95","session_id":"e51e0195-9c84-4e56-b272-a71ad93d54d2","uuid":"44ec7505-91e1-4a74-923d-5325d6a68ba7","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:59:17.201Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PLSSPmo4zLH2evoNAxsgP4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JcfwMvGjUNRpf6W8fNUts9","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for EMNLP 2026 ARR. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR scoring (Overall Assessment, half-point increments):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference acceptance\n- 3 = Findings acceptance\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5).\n\n## Paper summary\n\"Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis\"\n\nThe paper applies Mantel-Haenszel DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items), finding 31.3% of items show large temporal DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts — 142× above permutation null (z=622). \n\n4 contributions claimed:\n1. Temporal instability characterization (31.3% C-rate, 6-confound elimination, 6-benchmark replication)\n2. Ground truth meta-critique (web-overlap labels are fundamentally mismatched; enrichment≈1.0)\n3. Semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000)\n4. DIF as diagnostic lens (ρ≈0.997 but z=8.5 cohort bias)\n\nKey results: 6 benchmarks show 17.9-31.3% temporal DIF. Confound elimination covers architecture, ability gap, local independence, model non-independence, IRT θ, non-uniform DIF. Simpson's paradox shows domain improvement is NOT the driver (OR reverses from 1.55→0.73). Cross-subject matching Proposition 1 achieves ΔFPR=0.\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n1. How novel is applying MH-DIF to LLM benchmarks? Is this primarily \"applying existing psychometric method to new domain\" or does it contribute methodological novelty?\n2. The cross-subject matching (Proposition 1, ΔFPR=0) — is this a genuinely new methodological contribution or an obvious extension?\n3. The ground truth meta-critique (§4.5) — is the argument that web-overlap labels are fundamentally mismatched for item-level differential detection novel and impactful?\n4. The \"DIF as diagnostic lens\" framing — does this go beyond \"we found DIF exists\" to provide actionable insight?\n5. The semi-synthetic validation framework — is this reusable infrastructure or a one-off validation?\n6. Simpson's paradox finding (Table 4) — is this a genuine analytical insight or expected?\n\n### Related Work Assessment\n7. The Related Work section (§2) is quite brief (~1/3 page). Is it sufficient for EMNLP?\n8. Missing related work: \n   - Benchmark dynamics / temporal analysis literature (e.g., Kiela et al. 2021 \"Dynabench\")?\n   - IRT applications to NLP (e.g., Lalor et al. 2019, Rodriguez et al. 2021)?\n   - Contamination detection beyond Min-K%/ConStat/DVD (e.g., Golchin & Surdeanu 2023, Shi et al. 2024)?\n   - Benchmark saturation / ceiling effects literature?\n   - Educational measurement theory beyond DIF (e.g., measurement invariance, Rasch modeling)?\n9. Is the positioning against Li et al. [2024] web-overlap labels fair and accurate?\n10. Is the positioning against Growing Pains [Polo et al., 2024] adequate?\n11. Are there prior DIF applications to computational assessment that should be cited?\n\n### Contribution Assessment\n12. Does the paper pass the \"so what\" test? If 31% of items are unstable, what should the field DO differently?\n13. Is the \"instability ≠ invalidity\" narrative convincing, or does it undermine the paper's own importance?\n14. Are the 4 contributions appropriately scoped? Over-claimed or under-claimed?\n15. How does this compare to related concurrent work on benchmark contamination/degradation?\n\n### Writing and Presentation\n16. Is the paper well-written for the EMNLP audience? Are psychometric concepts adequately explained?\n17. Is the 8-page limit well-used? Any sections too compressed or too verbose?\n18. Are the tables and figures effective?\n19. Is the narrative arc (instability exists → not artifact → web-overlap is wrong → DIF is diagnostic) compelling?\n\n### References\nThe paper cites: Hendrycks et al. 2021, Holland & Thayer 1988, Holland & Wainer 1993, Li et al. 2024, Mantel & Haenszel 1959, Perlitz et al. 2024, Polo et al. 2024, Zieky 1993.\n- Only 8 references. Is this sufficient for EMNLP?\n- Are key citations missing?\n- Are all cited works real (no hallucinated citations)?\n\n## Output format\nStructure your review as:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, be specific]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, classify as Major or Minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Novelty Verdict\n[1 paragraph: is this sufficiently novel for EMNLP main conference?]\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n[Specific missing citations/areas]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (with justification referencing the EMNLP scale)\n\n### Confidence\n[1-5 scale, with justification]\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The bar is EMNLP main conference (top ~21%)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":46355,"cache_read_input_tokens":24148,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":46355},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e51e0195-9c84-4e56-b272-a71ad93d54d2","uuid":"dadfb448-fcd7-4bc0-a2d2-0ebfac82e10b"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a1bd33758c28ac3e8","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JcfwMvGjUNRpf6W8fNUts9","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for EMNLP 2026 ARR. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR scoring (Overall Assessment, half-point increments):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference acceptance\n- 3 = Findings acceptance\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5).\n\n## Paper summary\n\"Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis\"\n\nThe paper applies Mantel-Haenszel DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items), finding 31.3% of items show large temporal DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts — 142× above permutation null (z=622). \n\n4 contributions claimed:\n1. Temporal instability characterization (31.3% C-rate, 6-confound elimination, 6-benchmark replication)\n2. Ground truth meta-critique (web-overlap labels are fundamentally mismatched; enrichment≈1.0)\n3. Semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000)\n4. DIF as diagnostic lens (ρ≈0.997 but z=8.5 cohort bias)\n\nKey results: 6 benchmarks show 17.9-31.3% temporal DIF. Confound elimination covers architecture, ability gap, local independence, model non-independence, IRT θ, non-uniform DIF. Simpson's paradox shows domain improvement is NOT the driver (OR reverses from 1.55→0.73). Cross-subject matching Proposition 1 achieves ΔFPR=0.\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n1. How novel is applying MH-DIF to LLM benchmarks? Is this primarily \"applying existing psychometric method to new domain\" or does it contribute methodological novelty?\n2. The cross-subject matching (Proposition 1, ΔFPR=0) — is this a genuinely new methodological contribution or an obvious extension?\n3. The ground truth meta-critique (§4.5) — is the argument that web-overlap labels are fundamentally mismatched for item-level differential detection novel and impactful?\n4. The \"DIF as diagnostic lens\" framing — does this go beyond \"we found DIF exists\" to provide actionable insight?\n5. The semi-synthetic validation framework — is this reusable infrastructure or a one-off validation?\n6. Simpson's paradox finding (Table 4) — is this a genuine analytical insight or expected?\n\n### Related Work Assessment\n7. The Related Work section (§2) is quite brief (~1/3 page). Is it sufficient for EMNLP?\n8. Missing related work: \n   - Benchmark dynamics / temporal analysis literature (e.g., Kiela et al. 2021 \"Dynabench\")?\n   - IRT applications to NLP (e.g., Lalor et al. 2019, Rodriguez et al. 2021)?\n   - Contamination detection beyond Min-K%/ConStat/DVD (e.g., Golchin & Surdeanu 2023, Shi et al. 2024)?\n   - Benchmark saturation / ceiling effects literature?\n   - Educational measurement theory beyond DIF (e.g., measurement invariance, Rasch modeling)?\n9. Is the positioning against Li et al. [2024] web-overlap labels fair and accurate?\n10. Is the positioning against Growing Pains [Polo et al., 2024] adequate?\n11. Are there prior DIF applications to computational assessment that should be cited?\n\n### Contribution Assessment\n12. Does the paper pass the \"so what\" test? If 31% of items are unstable, what should the field DO differently?\n13. Is the \"instability ≠ invalidity\" narrative convincing, or does it undermine the paper's own importance?\n14. Are the 4 contributions appropriately scoped? Over-claimed or under-claimed?\n15. How does this compare to related concurrent work on benchmark contamination/degradation?\n\n### Writing and Presentation\n16. Is the paper well-written for the EMNLP audience? Are psychometric concepts adequately explained?\n17. Is the 8-page limit well-used? Any sections too compressed or too verbose?\n18. Are the tables and figures effective?\n19. Is the narrative arc (instability exists → not artifact → web-overlap is wrong → DIF is diagnostic) compelling?\n\n### References\nThe paper cites: Hendrycks et al. 2021, Holland & Thayer 1988, Holland & Wainer 1993, Li et al. 2024, Mantel & Haenszel 1959, Perlitz et al. 2024, Polo et al. 2024, Zieky 1993.\n- Only 8 references. Is this sufficient for EMNLP?\n- Are key citations missing?\n- Are all cited works real (no hallucinated citations)?\n\n## Output format\nStructure your review as:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, be specific]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, classify as Major or Minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Novelty Verdict\n[1 paragraph: is this sufficiently novel for EMNLP main conference?]\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n[Specific missing citations/areas]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (with justification referencing the EMNLP scale)\n\n### Confidence\n[1-5 scale, with justification]\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The bar is EMNLP main conference (top ~21%).","uuid":"9ededbe4-a186-48c9-9768-4a106c23b556","session_id":"e51e0195-9c84-4e56-b272-a71ad93d54d2"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for EMNLP 2026 ARR. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR scoring (Overall Assessment, half-point increments):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference acceptance\n- 3 = Findings acceptance\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5).\n\n## Paper summary\n\"Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis\"\n\nThe paper applies Mantel-Haenszel DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items), finding 31.3% of items show large temporal DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts — 142× above permutation null (z=622). \n\n4 contributions claimed:\n1. Temporal instability characterization (31.3% C-rate, 6-confound elimination, 6-benchmark replication)\n2. Ground truth meta-critique (web-overlap labels are fundamentally mismatched; enrichment≈1.0)\n3. Semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000)\n4. DIF as diagnostic lens (ρ≈0.997 but z=8.5 cohort bias)\n\nKey results: 6 benchmarks show 17.9-31.3% temporal DIF. Confound elimination covers architecture, ability gap, local independence, model non-independence, IRT θ, non-uniform DIF. Simpson's paradox shows domain improvement is NOT the driver (OR reverses from 1.55→0.73). Cross-subject matching Proposition 1 achieves ΔFPR=0.\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n1. How novel is applying MH-DIF to LLM benchmarks? Is this primarily \"applying existing psychometric method to new domain\" or does it contribute methodological novelty?\n2. The cross-subject matching (Proposition 1, ΔFPR=0) — is this a genuinely new methodological contribution or an obvious extension?\n3. The ground truth meta-critique (§4.5) — is the argument that web-overlap labels are fundamentally mismatched for item-level differential detection novel and impactful?\n4. The \"DIF as diagnostic lens\" framing — does this go beyond \"we found DIF exists\" to provide actionable insight?\n5. The semi-synthetic validation framework — is this reusable infrastructure or a one-off validation?\n6. Simpson's paradox finding (Table 4) — is this a genuine analytical insight or expected?\n\n### Related Work Assessment\n7. The Related Work section (§2) is quite brief (~1/3 page). Is it sufficient for EMNLP?\n8. Missing related work: \n   - Benchmark dynamics / temporal analysis literature (e.g., Kiela et al. 2021 \"Dynabench\")?\n   - IRT applications to NLP (e.g., Lalor et al. 2019, Rodriguez et al. 2021)?\n   - Contamination detection beyond Min-K%/ConStat/DVD (e.g., Golchin & Surdeanu 2023, Shi et al. 2024)?\n   - Benchmark saturation / ceiling effects literature?\n   - Educational measurement theory beyond DIF (e.g., measurement invariance, Rasch modeling)?\n9. Is the positioning against Li et al. [2024] web-overlap labels fair and accurate?\n10. Is the positioning against Growing Pains [Polo et al., 2024] adequate?\n11. Are there prior DIF applications to computational assessment that should be cited?\n\n### Contribution Assessment\n12. Does the paper pass the \"so what\" test? If 31% of items are unstable, what should the field DO differently?\n13. Is the \"instability ≠ invalidity\" narrative convincing, or does it undermine the paper's own importance?\n14. Are the 4 contributions appropriately scoped? Over-claimed or under-claimed?\n15. How does this compare to related concurrent work on benchmark contamination/degradation?\n\n### Writing and Presentation\n16. Is the paper well-written for the EMNLP audience? Are psychometric concepts adequately explained?\n17. Is the 8-page limit well-used? Any sections too compressed or too verbose?\n18. Are the tables and figures effective?\n19. Is the narrative arc (instability exists → not artifact → web-overlap is wrong → DIF is diagnostic) compelling?\n\n### References\nThe paper cites: Hendrycks et al. 2021, Holland & Thayer 1988, Holland & Wainer 1993, Li et al. 2024, Mantel & Haenszel 1959, Perlitz et al. 2024, Polo et al. 2024, Zieky 1993.\n- Only 8 references. Is this sufficient for EMNLP?\n- Are key citations missing?\n- Are all cited works real (no hallucinated citations)?\n\n## Output format\nStructure your review as:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, be specific]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, classify as Major or Minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Novelty Verdict\n[1 paragraph: is this sufficiently novel for EMNLP main conference?]\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n[Specific missing citations/areas]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (with justification referencing the EMNLP scale)\n\n### Confidence\n[1-5 scale, with justification]\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The bar is EMNLP main conference (top ~21%)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JcfwMvGjUNRpf6W8fNUts9","session_id":"e51e0195-9c84-4e56-b272-a71ad93d54d2","uuid":"e593910d-71e2-4a6f-8718-674431735ae9","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:59:25.716Z"}
