{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JcfwMvGjUNRpf6W8fNUts9","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me compile the review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper applies Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis from educational measurement to 5,227 LLMs evaluated on 27,125 items across six METABENCH benchmarks, finding 17.9--31.3% of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts. The paper argues this instability is diagnostic rather than destructive (rho=0.997 rank preservation), critiques web-overlap labels as inappropriate ground truth for differential detectors, proposes a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (DFPR=0), and characterizes DIF-flagged items as concentrating cross-generational discriminative signal with a structured difficulty-direction interaction. The writing is dense but clear, and the experimental controls are unusually thorough for this genre of paper.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Scale and thoroughness of controls.** The analysis covers 5,227 models x 12,508 MMLU items -- genuinely the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks. More importantly, the paper systematically eliminates six confounds (ability gap, model family structure, IRT theta conditioning, within-subject effects, local dependence, architecture heterogeneity). The matched-ability quintile analysis showing the temporal ability gap is negligible (d=-0.17) and nearest-neighbor matching reducing C% by only 2.4pp is convincing. This is substantially more rigorous than typical \"we applied method X and found result Y\" papers.\n\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique is genuinely insightful.** The distinction between global exposure and differential functioning (Section 4.3, Figure 1) is conceptually clean and empirically validated (enrichment=0.964, phi=0.012). The argument that web-overlap labels are categorically wrong for evaluating item-level differential detectors is not merely a criticism of one paper but identifies a systematic blind spot in the contamination detection literature's validation methodology. The dose-response curve in the semi-synthetic framework (DTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5) confirms DIF has detection power when differential behavior exists, ruling out the alternative explanation that the null result reflects DIF's insensitivity.\n\n3. **Simpson's paradox finding is non-trivial.** The reversal from OR=1.55 to OR=0.73 after controlling for item difficulty and discrimination (Table 4 / Section 4.2) is a genuine analytical contribution. It is not something one would expect a priori -- the naive hypothesis would be that domains where models improved most would show the most DIF. The finding that item properties, not accuracy gains, drive DIF classification substantively changes interpretation.\n\n4. **Cross-subject matching / Proposition 1.** While conceptually straightforward, the clean result (DFPR=0.000 with bootstrap CI [0.000, 0.000]) is practically valuable. The paper correctly identifies matching variable contamination as the central methodological challenge for semi-synthetic DIF evaluation and provides a principled solution. The fact that this has psychometric precedent (Nandakumar 1993, Donoghue & Allen 1993) is honestly disclosed.\n\n5. **\"Instability does not equal invalidity\" framing is mature.** The paper resists the temptation to claim MMLU is broken. The rho=0.997 finding plus the circularity ablation (DIF removal is the least disruptive non-random strategy) demonstrate intellectual honesty. The difficulty-direction interaction (rho=-0.597: gains on hard items, erosion on easy items) provides genuine mechanistic insight beyond \"DIF exists.\"\n\n6. **Cross-benchmark replication.** Extension to all six METABENCH benchmarks with a task-type dichotomy (knowledge/reasoning C% >= 26.9% vs procedural C% <= 18.9%) adds substantial generalizability and interpretive structure.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**Major:**\n\n1. **The core methodological contribution is primarily application, not innovation.** MH-DIF is a 1988 method. ETS classification is standard practice. The paper explicitly states \"we do not modify the MH procedure; our contribution is the application context and the empirical findings it reveals.\" For EMNLP main conference, the novelty bar requires either (a) a non-obvious methodological adaptation or (b) findings so surprising they shift community practice. The paper partially achieves (b) through the ground truth meta-critique and the Simpson's paradox, but the central DIF analysis itself is textbook application. Cross-subject matching has psychometric precedent (the paper cites Nandakumar 1993 and Donoghue 1993). Proposition 1, while useful, is mathematically trivial (removing a variable from the conditioning set eliminates its influence -- this follows from the definition). Labeling this as a \"Proposition\" with a formal proof overstates its intellectual contribution.\n\n2. **The \"so what\" is not fully resolved.** If 31% of items are unstable but rankings are preserved (rho=0.997), why should practitioners care? The audit protocol in Section 5.4 is a half-page sketch with vague thresholds (\"C% > 20% triggers item review\"). The paper does not demonstrate that acting on DIF findings improves any downstream task. The diagnostic lens framing is intellectually interesting but the paper stops short of showing that DIF-based analysis leads to better benchmarks, better evaluation, or better models. The difficulty-direction interaction is suggestive but remains descriptive.\n\n3. **Causal ambiguity limits impact.** The paper repeatedly and honestly acknowledges that DIF detects *that* items function differently but not *why*. This is scientifically appropriate but limits the actionable value. Is the instability driven by contamination? Training methodology shifts? Capability change? Data mixture composition? Without causal attribution, the 31% number is descriptive phenomenology. The GSM1K correlation (r=-0.478, N=33) is correctly marked as exploratory, but even if significant it only provides weak evidence for one causal pathway.\n\n4. **Related Work, while improved from earlier drafts, still has coverage gaps** (detailed below). The paper cites ~40 works in the bib file but the Related Work section is only about 1 page. For a paper that claims to bridge psychometrics and NLP evaluation, the engagement with both literatures should be deeper. In particular, the paper does not engage with:\n   - Perlitz et al. (2024) METABENCH itself -- the data source is cited but the paper's own psychometric findings are not discussed\n   - Benchmark saturation literature (Ott et al. 2022 is in the bib but not in Related Work text)\n   - MMLU-Pro (Wang et al. 2024) and other MMLU successors that already attempt to address item quality issues\n   - Measurement invariance testing beyond DIF (confirmatory factor analysis approaches, multi-group CFA) -- the paper cites Meredith 1993 and Vandenberg 2000 but does not discuss why CFA-based invariance testing was not pursued\n\n**Minor:**\n\n5. **Proposition 1's formal statement is imprecise.** The proposition states that DFPR=0 \"by construction,\" but this holds only under the assumption that cross-subject ability is independent of the injected contamination's effect on non-target items. In a multidimensional ability space (which MMLU clearly is, with 57 subjects), contamination in subject X could affect the matching variable computed from subjects Y,Z,... if there are strong latent factor correlations. The empirical result (DFPR=0.000) is convincing, but the theoretical guarantee should acknowledge this assumption explicitly.\n\n6. **The paper title mentions \"Psychometric Instability\" but the paper establishes that instability is compatible with validity.** This creates a slightly misleading first impression. A more precise title might emphasize \"measurement drift\" or \"differential item functioning\" rather than \"instability,\" which connotes unreliability.\n\n7. **Table 2 (cross-benchmark) lacks bootstrap CIs** for all benchmarks other than MMLU, making it unclear whether the observed differences between knowledge and procedural benchmarks are statistically significant or might overlap under uncertainty.\n\n8. **The 8-page limit is tightly used but the Discussion section is compressed.** Sections 5.1-5.4 occupy roughly 0.5 pages total, which is insufficient for a paper making four contributions and a recommended audit protocol. The implications for benchmark design, the relationship to concurrent work (Growing Pains), and the audit protocol all deserve more development. The Conclusion (Section 6) is a single paragraph that essentially restates the abstract.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Can you quantify the overlap between your DIF-C flagged items and the anchor items that Growing Pains (Habba et al. 2026) would select?** You claim complementarity but do not demonstrate it empirically. If their anchor items are overwhelmingly DIF-A items, that validates both approaches; if not, there is a tension that should be discussed.\n\n2. **What happens when you apply DIF analysis to 3-month or 1-month cohort windows rather than annual?** You show a 6-month window (Section 4.7, C%=34.35%), but finer granularity would help distinguish contamination (which could appear suddenly) from gradual capability drift.\n\n3. **The Simpson's paradox result (OR reversing from 1.55 to 0.73) is striking. Can you provide intuition for why controlling for item difficulty and discrimination reverses the direction?** Is this because harder items are both more likely to show DIF and more likely to appear in domains with larger accuracy improvements?\n\n4. **Why was MH-DIF chosen over logistic regression DIF as the primary method, given that logistic regression DIF captures both uniform and non-uniform DIF?** You note 24.8% additional non-uniform DIF in Limitations. If logistic regression DIF were primary, your headline number would be substantially higher. Is the choice driven by interpretability, historical convention, or computational cost?\n\n5. **The enrichment ratio of 0.964 between DIF and web-overlap labels could also be explained if DIF is simply measuring something unrelated to contamination entirely -- for example, measurement noise or item ambiguity that manifests differently across model generations.** How do you distinguish the \"DIF captures differential contamination that web-overlap misses\" interpretation from \"DIF captures something else entirely\"?\n\n6. **For the audit protocol (Section 5.4): what is the cost of a false positive under your recommended workflow?** If a benchmark maintainer flags 31% of items for review, what does \"review\" entail, and is this practical at scale?\n\n### Novelty Verdict\n\nThe paper's novelty profile is uneven. The core finding (applying MH-DIF to LLM benchmarks and finding 31% instability) is primarily \"well-known method applied to new domain\" -- rigorous and well-executed, but not methodologically novel. The ground truth meta-critique (Section 4.3) is the most novel intellectual contribution: identifying that the entire contamination detection literature's validation methodology is categorically mismatched for differential detection is an insight the community needs. The Simpson's paradox finding and the difficulty-direction interaction add genuine analytical novelty beyond \"we found DIF exists.\" The cross-subject matching framework, while mathematically straightforward, is practically useful and the paper honestly acknowledges its psychometric precedent. Taken together, the combination of (a) the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks, (b) the ground truth meta-critique, (c) the Simpson's paradox analysis, and (d) the reusable semi-synthetic framework constitutes a sufficient contribution for EMNLP, though it is closer to Findings-level than main conference level. The paper would benefit from stronger actionable implications -- currently, the \"so what\" resolution is the weakest link.\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n\n1. **Benchmark saturation literature**: Ott et al. (2022) is in the bib but not discussed in Related Work text. The connection between benchmark saturation and temporal DIF is natural and should be articulated.\n\n2. **MMLU successors**: MMLU-Pro (Wang et al. 2024) is in the bib but not in Related Work. Given that MMLU-Pro was explicitly designed to address quality issues in MMLU, the relationship to your findings deserves discussion.\n\n3. **HELM (Liang et al. 2023)**: In the bib but not in Related Work. HELM's holistic evaluation framework and its findings about benchmark reliability are relevant.\n\n4. **Confirmatory factor analysis / multi-group CFA approaches to measurement invariance**: The paper cites Meredith (1993) and Vandenberg (2000) but does not explain why these model-based approaches were not used. For the EMNLP audience, this gap is acceptable, but for psychometricians reading the paper it would raise questions.\n\n5. **Prior DIF applications to computer-based testing at scale**: Teresi (2007) is cited for health assessment, but there is a literature on DIF in large-scale computerized adaptive testing (e.g., Wainer 2000, \"Computerized Adaptive Testing\") that is more directly analogous.\n\n6. **Educational measurement's own debates about temporal DIF / item parameter drift**: This is a known phenomenon in educational testing (e.g., Goldstein 1983 on item parameter drift, Wells et al. 2002). The paper should acknowledge that temporal instability of items is not a new concept in psychometrics -- what is new is applying it to LLM benchmarks and the specific findings.\n\n7. **The reference count is adequate** (~40 works in the bib file). The in-text engagement could be deeper in places, but the breadth of coverage is acceptable for EMNLP.\n\n8. **Citation verification**: The bib entries appear real and correctly attributed. The 2026 citations (DVD, Self-Critique, Wang et al. fragility, Growing Pains, PSN-IRT, Hardy scalability, BHI, Maeda DIF-LLM) are all plausible arXiv preprints. No obviously hallucinated citations detected.\n\n### Scores\n- **Soundness: 4/5** -- The experimental design is thorough with strong controls. The six-confound elimination is rigorous. Minor concerns about Proposition 1's theoretical precision and the causal ambiguity.\n- **Excitement: 3/5** -- The ground truth meta-critique and Simpson's paradox are genuinely interesting. But the core contribution is application of existing psychometric methods to a new domain, and the actionable implications are underdeveloped. The \"so what\" is not fully answered.\n- **Reproducibility: 4/5** -- METABENCH is public data. MH-DIF is a standard procedure. Code is promised at anonymous link. The semi-synthetic framework is clearly described. Minor concern: some implementation details (exact quintile boundaries, BH correction implementation) are in the appendix.\n- **Overall Assessment: 3.0 (Findings)** -- The paper is technically sound, empirically thorough, and identifies a real phenomenon. The ground truth meta-critique is an insight the community needs. However, the core novelty is primarily \"apply existing psychometric method to new domain\" rather than methodological innovation. The paper does not fully resolve the \"so what\" question -- 31% instability that does not affect rankings invites the response \"then why should I care?\" The audit protocol is a sketch, not a demonstrated improvement. The difficulty-direction interaction and Simpson's paradox add genuine analytical novelty that lifts this above a straightforward application paper, but not enough for main conference given the ~21% acceptance rate. This is a strong Findings paper: well-executed, useful for the community, and the meta-critique should influence validation practices in contamination detection research.\n\n### Confidence\n**4/5 (High).** I have expertise in both educational measurement/psychometrics and NLP evaluation methodology. I am familiar with the DIF literature, the contamination detection literature, and the METABENCH dataset. My main uncertainty is whether the EMNLP reviewing pool would weight the \"novelty of application context\" more heavily than I do -- a more NLP-focused reviewer might find the psychometric framing more novel than I assess it to be. I am confident in the technical assessment."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a1bd33758c28ac3e8 (use SendMessage with to: 'a1bd33758c28ac3e8' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 56741\ntool_uses: 16\nduration_ms: 135001</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e51e0195-9c84-4e56-b272-a71ad93d54d2","uuid":"0cfa6143-0294-48f2-8783-5520188a5e56","timestamp":"2026-05-21T15:01:40.719Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for EMNLP 2026 ARR. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR scoring (Overall Assessment, half-point increments):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference acceptance\n- 3 = Findings acceptance\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5).\n\n## Paper summary\n\"Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis\"\n\nThe paper applies Mantel-Haenszel DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items), finding 31.3% of items show large temporal DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts — 142× above permutation null (z=622). \n\n4 contributions claimed:\n1. Temporal instability characterization (31.3% C-rate, 6-confound elimination, 6-benchmark replication)\n2. Ground truth meta-critique (web-overlap labels are fundamentally mismatched; enrichment≈1.0)\n3. Semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000)\n4. DIF as diagnostic lens (ρ≈0.997 but z=8.5 cohort bias)\n\nKey results: 6 benchmarks show 17.9-31.3% temporal DIF. Confound elimination covers architecture, ability gap, local independence, model non-independence, IRT θ, non-uniform DIF. Simpson's paradox shows domain improvement is NOT the driver (OR reverses from 1.55→0.73). Cross-subject matching Proposition 1 achieves ΔFPR=0.\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n1. How novel is applying MH-DIF to LLM benchmarks? Is this primarily \"applying existing psychometric method to new domain\" or does it contribute methodological novelty?\n2. The cross-subject matching (Proposition 1, ΔFPR=0) — is this a genuinely new methodological contribution or an obvious extension?\n3. The ground truth meta-critique (§4.5) — is the argument that web-overlap labels are fundamentally mismatched for item-level differential detection novel and impactful?\n4. The \"DIF as diagnostic lens\" framing — does this go beyond \"we found DIF exists\" to provide actionable insight?\n5. The semi-synthetic validation framework — is this reusable infrastructure or a one-off validation?\n6. Simpson's paradox finding (Table 4) — is this a genuine analytical insight or expected?\n\n### Related Work Assessment\n7. The Related Work section (§2) is quite brief (~1/3 page). Is it sufficient for EMNLP?\n8. Missing related work: \n   - Benchmark dynamics / temporal analysis literature (e.g., Kiela et al. 2021 \"Dynabench\")?\n   - IRT applications to NLP (e.g., Lalor et al. 2019, Rodriguez et al. 2021)?\n   - Contamination detection beyond Min-K%/ConStat/DVD (e.g., Golchin & Surdeanu 2023, Shi et al. 2024)?\n   - Benchmark saturation / ceiling effects literature?\n   - Educational measurement theory beyond DIF (e.g., measurement invariance, Rasch modeling)?\n9. Is the positioning against Li et al. [2024] web-overlap labels fair and accurate?\n10. Is the positioning against Growing Pains [Polo et al., 2024] adequate?\n11. Are there prior DIF applications to computational assessment that should be cited?\n\n### Contribution Assessment\n12. Does the paper pass the \"so what\" test? If 31% of items are unstable, what should the field DO differently?\n13. Is the \"instability ≠ invalidity\" narrative convincing, or does it undermine the paper's own importance?\n14. Are the 4 contributions appropriately scoped? Over-claimed or under-claimed?\n15. How does this compare to related concurrent work on benchmark contamination/degradation?\n\n### Writing and Presentation\n16. Is the paper well-written for the EMNLP audience? Are psychometric concepts adequately explained?\n17. Is the 8-page limit well-used? Any sections too compressed or too verbose?\n18. Are the tables and figures effective?\n19. Is the narrative arc (instability exists → not artifact → web-overlap is wrong → DIF is diagnostic) compelling?\n\n### References\nThe paper cites: Hendrycks et al. 2021, Holland & Thayer 1988, Holland & Wainer 1993, Li et al. 2024, Mantel & Haenszel 1959, Perlitz et al. 2024, Polo et al. 2024, Zieky 1993.\n- Only 8 references. Is this sufficient for EMNLP?\n- Are key citations missing?\n- Are all cited works real (no hallucinated citations)?\n\n## Output format\nStructure your review as:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, be specific]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, classify as Major or Minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Novelty Verdict\n[1 paragraph: is this sufficiently novel for EMNLP main conference?]\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n[Specific missing citations/areas]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (with justification referencing the EMNLP scale)\n\n### Confidence\n[1-5 scale, with justification]\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The bar is EMNLP main conference (top ~21%).","agentId":"a1bd33758c28ac3e8","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me compile the review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThis paper applies Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis from educational measurement to 5,227 LLMs evaluated on 27,125 items across six METABENCH benchmarks, finding 17.9--31.3% of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts. The paper argues this instability is diagnostic rather than destructive (rho=0.997 rank preservation), critiques web-overlap labels as inappropriate ground truth for differential detectors, proposes a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (DFPR=0), and characterizes DIF-flagged items as concentrating cross-generational discriminative signal with a structured difficulty-direction interaction. The writing is dense but clear, and the experimental controls are unusually thorough for this genre of paper.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Scale and thoroughness of controls.** The analysis covers 5,227 models x 12,508 MMLU items -- genuinely the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks. More importantly, the paper systematically eliminates six confounds (ability gap, model family structure, IRT theta conditioning, within-subject effects, local dependence, architecture heterogeneity). The matched-ability quintile analysis showing the temporal ability gap is negligible (d=-0.17) and nearest-neighbor matching reducing C% by only 2.4pp is convincing. This is substantially more rigorous than typical \"we applied method X and found result Y\" papers.\n\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique is genuinely insightful.** The distinction between global exposure and differential functioning (Section 4.3, Figure 1) is conceptually clean and empirically validated (enrichment=0.964, phi=0.012). The argument that web-overlap labels are categorically wrong for evaluating item-level differential detectors is not merely a criticism of one paper but identifies a systematic blind spot in the contamination detection literature's validation methodology. The dose-response curve in the semi-synthetic framework (DTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5) confirms DIF has detection power when differential behavior exists, ruling out the alternative explanation that the null result reflects DIF's insensitivity.\n\n3. **Simpson's paradox finding is non-trivial.** The reversal from OR=1.55 to OR=0.73 after controlling for item difficulty and discrimination (Table 4 / Section 4.2) is a genuine analytical contribution. It is not something one would expect a priori -- the naive hypothesis would be that domains where models improved most would show the most DIF. The finding that item properties, not accuracy gains, drive DIF classification substantively changes interpretation.\n\n4. **Cross-subject matching / Proposition 1.** While conceptually straightforward, the clean result (DFPR=0.000 with bootstrap CI [0.000, 0.000]) is practically valuable. The paper correctly identifies matching variable contamination as the central methodological challenge for semi-synthetic DIF evaluation and provides a principled solution. The fact that this has psychometric precedent (Nandakumar 1993, Donoghue & Allen 1993) is honestly disclosed.\n\n5. **\"Instability does not equal invalidity\" framing is mature.** The paper resists the temptation to claim MMLU is broken. The rho=0.997 finding plus the circularity ablation (DIF removal is the least disruptive non-random strategy) demonstrate intellectual honesty. The difficulty-direction interaction (rho=-0.597: gains on hard items, erosion on easy items) provides genuine mechanistic insight beyond \"DIF exists.\"\n\n6. **Cross-benchmark replication.** Extension to all six METABENCH benchmarks with a task-type dichotomy (knowledge/reasoning C% >= 26.9% vs procedural C% <= 18.9%) adds substantial generalizability and interpretive structure.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**Major:**\n\n1. **The core methodological contribution is primarily application, not innovation.** MH-DIF is a 1988 method. ETS classification is standard practice. The paper explicitly states \"we do not modify the MH procedure; our contribution is the application context and the empirical findings it reveals.\" For EMNLP main conference, the novelty bar requires either (a) a non-obvious methodological adaptation or (b) findings so surprising they shift community practice. The paper partially achieves (b) through the ground truth meta-critique and the Simpson's paradox, but the central DIF analysis itself is textbook application. Cross-subject matching has psychometric precedent (the paper cites Nandakumar 1993 and Donoghue 1993). Proposition 1, while useful, is mathematically trivial (removing a variable from the conditioning set eliminates its influence -- this follows from the definition). Labeling this as a \"Proposition\" with a formal proof overstates its intellectual contribution.\n\n2. **The \"so what\" is not fully resolved.** If 31% of items are unstable but rankings are preserved (rho=0.997), why should practitioners care? The audit protocol in Section 5.4 is a half-page sketch with vague thresholds (\"C% > 20% triggers item review\"). The paper does not demonstrate that acting on DIF findings improves any downstream task. The diagnostic lens framing is intellectually interesting but the paper stops short of showing that DIF-based analysis leads to better benchmarks, better evaluation, or better models. The difficulty-direction interaction is suggestive but remains descriptive.\n\n3. **Causal ambiguity limits impact.** The paper repeatedly and honestly acknowledges that DIF detects *that* items function differently but not *why*. This is scientifically appropriate but limits the actionable value. Is the instability driven by contamination? Training methodology shifts? Capability change? Data mixture composition? Without causal attribution, the 31% number is descriptive phenomenology. The GSM1K correlation (r=-0.478, N=33) is correctly marked as exploratory, but even if significant it only provides weak evidence for one causal pathway.\n\n4. **Related Work, while improved from earlier drafts, still has coverage gaps** (detailed below). The paper cites ~40 works in the bib file but the Related Work section is only about 1 page. For a paper that claims to bridge psychometrics and NLP evaluation, the engagement with both literatures should be deeper. In particular, the paper does not engage with:\n   - Perlitz et al. (2024) METABENCH itself -- the data source is cited but the paper's own psychometric findings are not discussed\n   - Benchmark saturation literature (Ott et al. 2022 is in the bib but not in Related Work text)\n   - MMLU-Pro (Wang et al. 2024) and other MMLU successors that already attempt to address item quality issues\n   - Measurement invariance testing beyond DIF (confirmatory factor analysis approaches, multi-group CFA) -- the paper cites Meredith 1993 and Vandenberg 2000 but does not discuss why CFA-based invariance testing was not pursued\n\n**Minor:**\n\n5. **Proposition 1's formal statement is imprecise.** The proposition states that DFPR=0 \"by construction,\" but this holds only under the assumption that cross-subject ability is independent of the injected contamination's effect on non-target items. In a multidimensional ability space (which MMLU clearly is, with 57 subjects), contamination in subject X could affect the matching variable computed from subjects Y,Z,... if there are strong latent factor correlations. The empirical result (DFPR=0.000) is convincing, but the theoretical guarantee should acknowledge this assumption explicitly.\n\n6. **The paper title mentions \"Psychometric Instability\" but the paper establishes that instability is compatible with validity.** This creates a slightly misleading first impression. A more precise title might emphasize \"measurement drift\" or \"differential item functioning\" rather than \"instability,\" which connotes unreliability.\n\n7. **Table 2 (cross-benchmark) lacks bootstrap CIs** for all benchmarks other than MMLU, making it unclear whether the observed differences between knowledge and procedural benchmarks are statistically significant or might overlap under uncertainty.\n\n8. **The 8-page limit is tightly used but the Discussion section is compressed.** Sections 5.1-5.4 occupy roughly 0.5 pages total, which is insufficient for a paper making four contributions and a recommended audit protocol. The implications for benchmark design, the relationship to concurrent work (Growing Pains), and the audit protocol all deserve more development. The Conclusion (Section 6) is a single paragraph that essentially restates the abstract.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Can you quantify the overlap between your DIF-C flagged items and the anchor items that Growing Pains (Habba et al. 2026) would select?** You claim complementarity but do not demonstrate it empirically. If their anchor items are overwhelmingly DIF-A items, that validates both approaches; if not, there is a tension that should be discussed.\n\n2. **What happens when you apply DIF analysis to 3-month or 1-month cohort windows rather than annual?** You show a 6-month window (Section 4.7, C%=34.35%), but finer granularity would help distinguish contamination (which could appear suddenly) from gradual capability drift.\n\n3. **The Simpson's paradox result (OR reversing from 1.55 to 0.73) is striking. Can you provide intuition for why controlling for item difficulty and discrimination reverses the direction?** Is this because harder items are both more likely to show DIF and more likely to appear in domains with larger accuracy improvements?\n\n4. **Why was MH-DIF chosen over logistic regression DIF as the primary method, given that logistic regression DIF captures both uniform and non-uniform DIF?** You note 24.8% additional non-uniform DIF in Limitations. If logistic regression DIF were primary, your headline number would be substantially higher. Is the choice driven by interpretability, historical convention, or computational cost?\n\n5. **The enrichment ratio of 0.964 between DIF and web-overlap labels could also be explained if DIF is simply measuring something unrelated to contamination entirely -- for example, measurement noise or item ambiguity that manifests differently across model generations.** How do you distinguish the \"DIF captures differential contamination that web-overlap misses\" interpretation from \"DIF captures something else entirely\"?\n\n6. **For the audit protocol (Section 5.4): what is the cost of a false positive under your recommended workflow?** If a benchmark maintainer flags 31% of items for review, what does \"review\" entail, and is this practical at scale?\n\n### Novelty Verdict\n\nThe paper's novelty profile is uneven. The core finding (applying MH-DIF to LLM benchmarks and finding 31% instability) is primarily \"well-known method applied to new domain\" -- rigorous and well-executed, but not methodologically novel. The ground truth meta-critique (Section 4.3) is the most novel intellectual contribution: identifying that the entire contamination detection literature's validation methodology is categorically mismatched for differential detection is an insight the community needs. The Simpson's paradox finding and the difficulty-direction interaction add genuine analytical novelty beyond \"we found DIF exists.\" The cross-subject matching framework, while mathematically straightforward, is practically useful and the paper honestly acknowledges its psychometric precedent. Taken together, the combination of (a) the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks, (b) the ground truth meta-critique, (c) the Simpson's paradox analysis, and (d) the reusable semi-synthetic framework constitutes a sufficient contribution for EMNLP, though it is closer to Findings-level than main conference level. The paper would benefit from stronger actionable implications -- currently, the \"so what\" resolution is the weakest link.\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n\n1. **Benchmark saturation literature**: Ott et al. (2022) is in the bib but not discussed in Related Work text. The connection between benchmark saturation and temporal DIF is natural and should be articulated.\n\n2. **MMLU successors**: MMLU-Pro (Wang et al. 2024) is in the bib but not in Related Work. Given that MMLU-Pro was explicitly designed to address quality issues in MMLU, the relationship to your findings deserves discussion.\n\n3. **HELM (Liang et al. 2023)**: In the bib but not in Related Work. HELM's holistic evaluation framework and its findings about benchmark reliability are relevant.\n\n4. **Confirmatory factor analysis / multi-group CFA approaches to measurement invariance**: The paper cites Meredith (1993) and Vandenberg (2000) but does not explain why these model-based approaches were not used. For the EMNLP audience, this gap is acceptable, but for psychometricians reading the paper it would raise questions.\n\n5. **Prior DIF applications to computer-based testing at scale**: Teresi (2007) is cited for health assessment, but there is a literature on DIF in large-scale computerized adaptive testing (e.g., Wainer 2000, \"Computerized Adaptive Testing\") that is more directly analogous.\n\n6. **Educational measurement's own debates about temporal DIF / item parameter drift**: This is a known phenomenon in educational testing (e.g., Goldstein 1983 on item parameter drift, Wells et al. 2002). The paper should acknowledge that temporal instability of items is not a new concept in psychometrics -- what is new is applying it to LLM benchmarks and the specific findings.\n\n7. **The reference count is adequate** (~40 works in the bib file). The in-text engagement could be deeper in places, but the breadth of coverage is acceptable for EMNLP.\n\n8. **Citation verification**: The bib entries appear real and correctly attributed. The 2026 citations (DVD, Self-Critique, Wang et al. fragility, Growing Pains, PSN-IRT, Hardy scalability, BHI, Maeda DIF-LLM) are all plausible arXiv preprints. No obviously hallucinated citations detected.\n\n### Scores\n- **Soundness: 4/5** -- The experimental design is thorough with strong controls. The six-confound elimination is rigorous. Minor concerns about Proposition 1's theoretical precision and the causal ambiguity.\n- **Excitement: 3/5** -- The ground truth meta-critique and Simpson's paradox are genuinely interesting. But the core contribution is application of existing psychometric methods to a new domain, and the actionable implications are underdeveloped. The \"so what\" is not fully answered.\n- **Reproducibility: 4/5** -- METABENCH is public data. MH-DIF is a standard procedure. Code is promised at anonymous link. The semi-synthetic framework is clearly described. Minor concern: some implementation details (exact quintile boundaries, BH correction implementation) are in the appendix.\n- **Overall Assessment: 3.0 (Findings)** -- The paper is technically sound, empirically thorough, and identifies a real phenomenon. The ground truth meta-critique is an insight the community needs. However, the core novelty is primarily \"apply existing psychometric method to new domain\" rather than methodological innovation. The paper does not fully resolve the \"so what\" question -- 31% instability that does not affect rankings invites the response \"then why should I care?\" The audit protocol is a sketch, not a demonstrated improvement. The difficulty-direction interaction and Simpson's paradox add genuine analytical novelty that lifts this above a straightforward application paper, but not enough for main conference given the ~21% acceptance rate. This is a strong Findings paper: well-executed, useful for the community, and the meta-critique should influence validation practices in contamination detection research.\n\n### Confidence\n**4/5 (High).** I have expertise in both educational measurement/psychometrics and NLP evaluation methodology. I am familiar with the DIF literature, the contamination detection literature, and the METABENCH dataset. My main uncertainty is whether the EMNLP reviewing pool would weight the \"novelty of application context\" more heavily than I do -- a more NLP-focused reviewer might find the psychometric framing more novel than I assess it to be. I am confident in the technical assessment."}],"totalDurationMs":135001,"totalTokens":56741,"totalToolUseCount":16,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8643,"cache_read_input_tokens":44275,"output_tokens":3822,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8643},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3822,"cache_read_input_tokens":44275,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8643,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8643,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":3}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a5a2c31e105f25591","tool_use_id":"toolu_017Ve6VeYsmSXUuKEKZ2Ge95","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":78589,"tool_uses":23,"duration_ms":170891},"uuid":"888b0b5d-f34e-4c16-b19a-ba8a273f4068","session_id":"e51e0195-9c84-4e56-b272-a71ad93d54d2"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017Ve6VeYsmSXUuKEKZ2Ge95","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I notice the abstract does NOT mention \"z=622\" or \"142x\" -- those were in the task summary but not the actual paper. The paper uses \"45-174x\" above random baselines. Let me now compile the review based on my thorough reading.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper applies Mantel-Haenszel DIF analysis to six METABENCH benchmarks, finding 17.9--31.3% of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts. The paper pivots the DIF literature's usual \"detection tool\" framing into a \"diagnostic lens\" for characterizing benchmark measurement drift. The confound elimination is unusually thorough (six alternative explanations tested), and the semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching is a clean methodological contribution. The core tension is that the paper applies an unmodified 1988 psychometric procedure to a new domain, and the novelty rests entirely on the empirical findings and the ground truth meta-critique.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Exceptionally thorough confound elimination (Section 4.3, 4.4).** Six potential alternative explanations are systematically tested and ruled out: ability confounding (d=-0.17, matched C%=28.9% vs 31.3%), model family inflation (graduated de-duplication, C% at or below random subsample at every K), IRT theta-conditioning (r=0.9986, FPR=0.312), within-subject effects (C%=38.1%), aggregate ranking impact (rho=0.997), and local item dependence (Q3=0.009). The contrast between temporal matching (2.4pp reduction) and within-family matching (33pp reduction for Llama-2 vs Llama-3) is particularly informative and demonstrates careful thinking about when ability confounding matters.\n\n2. **Cross-subject matching achieves clean DFPR=0.000 (Section 3.2, Proposition 1).** The insight that the MH matching variable absorbs injected contamination signal under within-subject matching is subtle and practically important. The empirical confirmation across all five MMLU domains with bootstrap CIs is well-executed. This is a genuinely useful methodological contribution for anyone doing semi-synthetic DIF validation.\n\n3. **Ground truth meta-critique is well-argued and empirically grounded (Section 4.2).** The enrichment=0.964, Jaccard=0.206, phi=0.012 results convincingly demonstrate that web-overlap labels and DIF flags capture orthogonal phenomena. The conceptual argument (global exposure vs. differential functioning) is sharp, and the semi-synthetic dose-response (Figure 2) provides the necessary control showing DIF can detect differential contamination when it is present. This is a genuine contribution to the contamination detection literature.\n\n4. **Scale of analysis is unprecedented.** 5,227 models x 12,508 MMLU items, replicated across 6 benchmarks totaling 27,125 items. The METABENCH response matrix provides an unusually rich foundation for this work.\n\n5. **Simpson's paradox result is compelling (Section 4.1).** The reversal from OR=1.55 (aggregate) to OR=0.73 (item-level controls) with LR chi-squared=647 is a clean demonstration that item properties, not accuracy gains, drive DIF classification. This effectively addresses the \"models just got better\" objection.\n\n6. **Difficulty-direction interaction (rho=-0.597) provides interpretable structure.** The finding that hard items favor 2024 models while easy items favor 2023 models goes beyond merely counting DIF items and provides a substantive characterization of what changed.\n\n7. **Honest handling of limitations.** The paper acknowledges that DIF detects \"that\" items function differently but not \"why\" (Section Limitations). The GSM1K correlation is appropriately hedged as exploratory (N=33). The pivot from \"contamination detection\" to \"psychometric characterization\" is intellectually honest.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **[Major] Proposition 1 is presented as a formal result but is really just an observation about variable construction.** The \"proof\" is one sentence: since the tested item belongs to subject s, it does not contribute to M_i^{(-s)}. This is true by construction for the *injected items*, but the claim of DFPR=0 requires an additional unstated assumption: that injecting contamination into subject s does not change the *strata assignments* for items in other subjects through altered total scores on the non-s items. In practice, if a model's performance on subject s items changes, its total score on the full benchmark changes, which could change its quintile assignment for items in subjects other than s. The cross-subject matching variable M_i^{(-s)} is unaffected by injection into s, but M_i^{(-s')} for s' != s IS affected by injection into s (because items in s are included in M_i^{(-s')}). When injecting into ALL subjects simultaneously (as in a realistic contamination scenario), the guarantee breaks down. The paper only injects into one subject at a time (Section 3.2), which is acknowledged implicitly but should be stated explicitly as a limitation of the framework.\n\n2. **[Major] The \"31% temporal DIF\" framing conflates genuine DIF with unresolvable impact.** The paper acknowledges that three structural improvement attempts failed to reduce C% below ~28-29% (decisions.yaml D005, D008, D010). The matched-ability analysis shows C% drops from 31.3% to 28.9% -- a modest reduction. But the paper does not adequately grapple with what this 28.9% *means*. In educational testing, DIF is concerning precisely because it indicates unfairness. Here, the paper argues DIF is \"diagnostic, not destructive\" and that \"instability != invalidity.\" But then what IS the actionable implication? The audit protocol (Section 5.4) recommends flagging items when C% > 20%, but since C% is 31% and the authors simultaneously argue this doesn't matter for rankings (rho=0.997), the protocol's decision-theoretic grounding is unclear.\n\n3. **[Major] K=5 strata may be insufficient for this population.** The paper uses 5 equiprobable strata for MH conditioning, citing ETS recommended practice (Donoghue 1993, Clauser 1998). However, these recommendations were developed for human test-taker populations with N=200-2000 and roughly normal ability distributions. With N=1,887 (1,080+807) and possibly multimodal ability distributions (due to model families clustering at different ability levels), K=5 may undersmooth the ability distribution and fail to adequately condition on ability. The IRT theta-conditioning with K=20 strata (Appendix B) yielded FPR=0.312 vs 0.314 for K=5, suggesting insensitivity -- but this could indicate that the *problem* is not the number of strata but the unidimensional nature of the conditioning variable. The paper should report sensitivity to K={5, 10, 15, 20} systematically in the main text rather than only reporting K=20 in the appendix.\n\n4. **[Major] The permutation test (plan_029) was abandoned.** Per D025 in decisions.yaml, the planned 1000-permutation null distribution for C% was abandoned after 4 implementation failures. The random-split control (C%=0.18%) is offered as a substitute, but it is NOT equivalent: a random split within the 2023 cohort tests a different null hypothesis (no group differences within a homogeneous cohort) than temporal permutation (no temporal structure in the observed DIF pattern). The \"45-174x above random baselines\" ratios in the abstract compare observed C% to within-cohort random splits, not to a proper permutation null. This is a meaningful gap: the random-split null is trivially expected to yield near-zero C% because both groups are from the same time period. A permutation null that shuffles temporal labels would provide a much more informative baseline.\n\n5. **[Minor] Semi-synthetic injection mechanism (incorrect->correct flipping) is a simplification that may overestimate detection power.** Real contamination could produce more subtle effects: partial memorization, correct-answer bias in multiple-choice format, or improved chain-of-thought that happens to reach correct answers. The paper acknowledges this in limitations but the DTPR=+0.508 headline number should be contextualized as an upper bound for this specific injection mechanism.\n\n6. **[Minor] Cross-benchmark independence (Table 5).** All six benchmarks share the same model population from METABENCH. The models evaluated on MMLU are largely the same models evaluated on GSM8K, HellaSwag, etc. While the items are different, the model-level correlation structure is shared. The claim of \"independent replication across 6 benchmarks\" should be hedged -- this is replication across item sets with shared examinees, not fully independent replication.\n\n7. **[Minor] GSM1K correlation (r=-0.478, N=33).** The paper appropriately hedges this as exploratory, but the 95% CI [-0.71, -0.16] is wide enough to include both substantial and negligible effects. The partial correlation (r=-0.385, p=0.027) barely survives the significance threshold. With N=33 and multiple statistical tests reported (Pearson, Spearman, partial, permutation, leave-one-out), there is some risk of selective reporting despite the robustness checks.\n\n8. **[Minor] The paper uses \"FPR\" in two different senses.** In Section 4.2, FPR(clean) = 0.318 is the rate at which DIF flags items that Li et al. classify as \"clean\" -- but the paper's own argument is that these labels are wrong. In the semi-synthetic framework, DFPR measures false positive inflation from injection. Using the same abbreviation for conceptually different quantities is confusing.\n\n9. **[Minor] Bootstrap CI [29.4%, 38.1%] is asymmetric around 31.3%.** The upper tail extends 6.8pp above the point estimate while the lower tail is only 1.9pp below. This suggests the bootstrap distribution is right-skewed, which could indicate sensitivity to specific item subsets. The paper does not discuss this asymmetry.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. How does Proposition 1's DFPR=0 guarantee behave when contamination is injected into multiple subjects simultaneously? The cross-subject matching variable for subject s is unaffected by injection into s, but it IS affected by injection into all other subjects.\n\n2. The ability-quintile comparison yields C%=2.9%. This is considerably above the random-split C%=0.18%. What explains this 16x difference? If ability differences alone produce 2.9% DIF, and the temporal cohorts have a small ability gap (d=-0.17), how do you decompose the 31.3% into ability-driven vs. genuinely temporal components? The matched-ability analysis (28.9%) suggests only 2.4pp is ability-driven, but the ability-quintile result seems to suggest ability differences can produce non-trivial DIF even within the same cohort.\n\n3. The 6-month window analysis (Section 4.7) shows C%=34.35%, which is *higher* than the full-year C%=31.3%. Is this counterintuitive? Shouldn't a shorter temporal window show less DIF? How do you explain this?\n\n4. The logistic regression reporting OR=1.55 -> OR=0.73 (Simpson's paradox): what are the sample sizes, degrees of freedom, and confidence intervals for these ORs? Is the reversed OR=0.73 practically meaningful or just statistically significant with N=12,508?\n\n5. Why were only 5 of 57 MMLU subjects used for within-subject DIF analysis (Section 4.3)? How were they selected? Could selection bias affect the 38.1% average?\n\n6. The paper states 92.1% concordance between MH and logistic regression DIF (Section 4.3). What happens to the 7.9% discordant items? Are these the ones driving the non-uniform DIF finding (24.8%)?\n\n### Missing Experiments Assessment\n\n**Adequately covered:**\n- plan_001 (Temporal DIF on MMLU): Thoroughly covered as the main result with extensive robustness checks.\n- plan_011 (Semi-synthetic): Core validation in Section 4.5, well-executed.\n- plan_010 (Architecture stratified): Covered in Appendix A via the 99.98% decoder-only observation.\n- plan_028 (Multi-benchmark): Table 5, all six METABENCH benchmarks.\n- plan_030 (Simpson's paradox): Table 4 / Section 4.1, compelling result.\n- plan_032 (DIF-cleaned demo): Section 4.7, rank shift analysis.\n- plan_033 (Subject concentration): Section 4.1, domain uniformity.\n- plan_004 (Cross-benchmark GSM8K): Section 4.7, including GSM1K correlation.\n- plan_031 (Temporal granularity): Section 4.7, 6-month window.\n\n**Missing but acknowledged:**\n- plan_029 (Permutation null): Abandoned (D025). Partially compensated by random-split control and cross-benchmark replication, but the formal permutation null would have been valuable.\n- plan_005 (Bifactor IRT): Killed after pre-check (D010). Mentioned in Limitations.\n- plan_002 (RL robustness): N=13, directional only. Appropriately demoted.\n\n**Missing and it matters:**\n- plan_003a/b (ConStat/DVD direct evaluation): Never executed. The paper critiques web-overlap labels but does not actually run ConStat or DVD on the METABENCH data to directly compare detection patterns. The enrichment=0.964 result is compelling but a head-to-head comparison of what DIF flags vs. what ConStat flags would have been the single most informative missing experiment. The paper partially addresses this by arguing that any detector validated against web-overlap labels inherits the same blind spot, but this is a logical argument rather than an empirical demonstration.\n- plan_029 (Permutation null): The absence weakens the \"45-174x above random baseline\" claim. A within-cohort random split is not a proper temporal permutation null.\n\n**Missing but doesn't significantly matter:**\n- plan_007 (Cumulative Bayesian DIF): Simulation-only, supplementary.\n- plan_008 (Graceful degradation): Supplementary analysis.\n- plan_009 (Item-Focused Trees): Alternative method, robustness check.\n\n### Scores\n\n- **Soundness: 3.5/5.** The MH-DIF application is methodologically correct and the confound elimination is thorough. The main soundness concerns are: (a) the abandoned permutation null means the primary comparison is against a weak baseline, (b) Proposition 1's guarantee is narrower than presented, and (c) the K=5 stratification adequacy is not fully justified for this population. None of these are fatal, but they introduce uncertainty about the quantitative precision of the headline numbers.\n\n- **Excitement: 3/5.** The empirical finding (31% temporal DIF) is interesting and practically relevant for the LLM evaluation community. The ground truth meta-critique has field-wide implications. However, the method is entirely standard (unmodified MH-DIF from 1988), the actionable implications are underdeveloped (the audit protocol is sketched but not validated), and the \"diagnostic lens\" framing, while intellectually honest, reduces the urgency of the findings (if rho=0.997, why should practitioners care?). The difficulty-direction interaction (rho=-0.597) is the most exciting individual finding but receives limited space.\n\n- **Reproducibility: 4/5.** METABENCH is publicly available, cohort definitions are clearly specified (2023 vs 2024 by release date), MH-DIF is a standard procedure with well-defined parameters (K=5, ETS thresholds). The paper promises code release. Minor gaps: the exact model-to-year mapping criteria are not fully specified (what constitutes \"release date\"?), and the bootstrap/seed details for the semi-synthetic framework could be more explicit.\n\n- **Overall Assessment: 3.5 (Borderline Conference).** The paper makes a genuine empirical contribution at unprecedented scale, with thorough confound elimination and a clean ground truth meta-critique. The cross-benchmark replication (6 benchmarks) and Simpson's paradox analysis strengthen the work considerably. However, the novelty is limited to applying an existing method to a new domain (no algorithmic contribution), the abandoned permutation null weakens the statistical argument, and the actionability gap (high instability but no ranking impact) creates a \"so what?\" tension that the audit protocol only partially resolves. The paper is above the Findings threshold (3.0) because the empirical findings are robust and the meta-critique has implications beyond this paper, but reaching Conference acceptance (4.0) would require either (a) a proper permutation null to replace the weak random-split baseline, (b) a head-to-head empirical comparison with ConStat/DVD rather than a logical argument, or (c) stronger development of the actionable implications (e.g., demonstrating that the audit protocol actually improves benchmark maintenance in a case study).\n\n### Confidence\n\n**4/5.** I have expertise in psychometric methods (including MH-DIF) and LLM evaluation methodology. I read the full paper, appendices, and supplementary materials carefully. I am confident in my assessment of the statistical methodology and experimental design. My uncertainty is primarily around the EMNLP evaluation community's appetite for \"characterization without action\" papers -- the practical impact question is partially a community norms judgment rather than a technical one."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a5a2c31e105f25591 (use SendMessage with to: 'a5a2c31e105f25591' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 82122\ntool_uses: 23\nduration_ms: 170893</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e51e0195-9c84-4e56-b272-a71ad93d54d2","uuid":"0225661e-3d8a-413c-a6be-d5ff54866a6f","timestamp":"2026-05-21T15:01:50.388Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for EMNLP 2026 ARR. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. \n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 ARR scoring (Overall Assessment, half-point increments):\n- 5 = Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference acceptance\n- 3 = Findings acceptance\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nAcceptance rate ~21%. Be calibrated accordingly.\n\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5).\n\n## Paper summary\n\"Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis\"\n\nThe paper applies Mantel-Haenszel DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items), finding 31.3% of items show large temporal DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts — 142× above permutation null (z=622). It makes 4 contributions:\n1. Temporal instability characterization (31.3% C-rate, robust to 6 confounds)\n2. Ground truth meta-critique (web-overlap labels are mismatched for DIF; enrichment≈1.0, χ²=1.28 p=0.258)\n3. Semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000, portable to GSM8K with ΔTPR=+0.818)\n4. DIF as diagnostic lens (ρ≈0.997 ranking stability but z=8.5 cohort bias upon DIF removal)\n\nReplicates across 6 benchmarks (17.9-31.3%). Confound elimination covers: architecture (99.98% decoder-only), ability gap (d=-0.17, 2.4pp confound), local independence (Q3=0.009), model non-independence (family-cap C%≤random at every K), IRT θ-conditioning (r=0.9986), non-uniform DIF (24.8% additional).\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n### Methodology\n1. Is MH-DIF correctly applied to LLM benchmarks? Any violations of MH assumptions (e.g., treating models as \"examinees\", binary response matrix validity)?\n2. Is the semi-synthetic validation framework (§3.2) sound? Does incorrect→correct flipping adequately model real contamination?\n3. Is Proposition 1 (cross-subject matching → ΔFPR=0) correctly stated and proven? Is the \"proof\" rigorous or just empirical?\n4. Are the 5 CTT total-score strata sufficient for MH conditioning, or could finer stratification change results?\n\n### Experiments\n5. Is the confound elimination (§4.3) thorough? Any missing confounds?\n6. Is the permutation test (§4.2) properly constructed? 1000 permutations sufficient?\n7. Simpson's paradox analysis (Table 4): Are the logistic regression models properly specified? Is R²=0.0007 the right metric?\n8. Cross-benchmark replication (Table 5): Are the 6 benchmarks independent enough? Do they share models?\n9. GSM1K correlation (r=-0.478, N=33): Is this appropriately hedged?\n10. DIF-cleaned demonstration (§4.7): Is z=8.5 cohort asymmetry convincing?\n11. Any statistical issues (multiple comparisons, selective reporting, p-hacking risk)?\n12. Missing experiments that would strengthen or weaken claims?\n\n### Experiment coverage\nThe experiment plan included these experiments. Check which are adequately covered vs missing:\n- Temporal DIF on MMLU (plan_001) — should be main result\n- RL robustness (plan_002) — N=13, directional only\n- ConStat/DVD eval (plan_003a/b) — not executed, cited from literature\n- Cross-benchmark GSM8K (plan_004) — should include semi-synthetic + GSM1K correlation\n- Bifactor IRT (plan_005) — killed, mentioned in Limitations\n- Cumulative Bayesian DIF (plan_007) — simulation in appendix\n- Graceful degradation (plan_008) — in §4.7/appendix\n- Item-Focused Trees (plan_009) — appendix\n- Architecture stratified (plan_010) — §4.3\n- Semi-synthetic (plan_011) — §4.6\n- Multi-benchmark (plan_028) — Table 5\n- Permutation null (plan_029) — Table 3\n- Simpson's paradox (plan_030) — Table 4\n- Temporal granularity (plan_031) — §4.4\n- DIF-cleaned demo (plan_032) — §4.7\n- Subject concentration (plan_033) — §4.1\n\n### Reproducibility\n13. Is there enough detail to reproduce (data source, code, parameters)?\n14. METABENCH is public, but are the exact cohort definitions reproducible?\n\n### Limitations assessment\n15. Are the 3 stated limitations (1D IRT misspecification, simplified injection, RL underpowered) the right ones? Missing limitations?\n\n## Output format\nStructure your review as:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, be specific with section/table references]\n\n### Weaknesses  \n[Numbered list, be specific. Classify each as Major or Minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Experiments Assessment\n[Which planned experiments are adequately covered, which are missing, and does it matter?]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5  \n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (with justification referencing the EMNLP scale)\n\n### Confidence\n[1-5 scale, with justification]\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Ground every criticism in specific evidence from the paper.","agentId":"a5a2c31e105f25591","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I notice the abstract does NOT mention \"z=622\" or \"142x\" -- those were in the task summary but not the actual paper. The paper uses \"45-174x\" above random baselines. Let me now compile the review based on my thorough reading.\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper applies Mantel-Haenszel DIF analysis to six METABENCH benchmarks, finding 17.9--31.3% of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts. The paper pivots the DIF literature's usual \"detection tool\" framing into a \"diagnostic lens\" for characterizing benchmark measurement drift. The confound elimination is unusually thorough (six alternative explanations tested), and the semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching is a clean methodological contribution. The core tension is that the paper applies an unmodified 1988 psychometric procedure to a new domain, and the novelty rests entirely on the empirical findings and the ground truth meta-critique.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Exceptionally thorough confound elimination (Section 4.3, 4.4).** Six potential alternative explanations are systematically tested and ruled out: ability confounding (d=-0.17, matched C%=28.9% vs 31.3%), model family inflation (graduated de-duplication, C% at or below random subsample at every K), IRT theta-conditioning (r=0.9986, FPR=0.312), within-subject effects (C%=38.1%), aggregate ranking impact (rho=0.997), and local item dependence (Q3=0.009). The contrast between temporal matching (2.4pp reduction) and within-family matching (33pp reduction for Llama-2 vs Llama-3) is particularly informative and demonstrates careful thinking about when ability confounding matters.\n\n2. **Cross-subject matching achieves clean DFPR=0.000 (Section 3.2, Proposition 1).** The insight that the MH matching variable absorbs injected contamination signal under within-subject matching is subtle and practically important. The empirical confirmation across all five MMLU domains with bootstrap CIs is well-executed. This is a genuinely useful methodological contribution for anyone doing semi-synthetic DIF validation.\n\n3. **Ground truth meta-critique is well-argued and empirically grounded (Section 4.2).** The enrichment=0.964, Jaccard=0.206, phi=0.012 results convincingly demonstrate that web-overlap labels and DIF flags capture orthogonal phenomena. The conceptual argument (global exposure vs. differential functioning) is sharp, and the semi-synthetic dose-response (Figure 2) provides the necessary control showing DIF can detect differential contamination when it is present. This is a genuine contribution to the contamination detection literature.\n\n4. **Scale of analysis is unprecedented.** 5,227 models x 12,508 MMLU items, replicated across 6 benchmarks totaling 27,125 items. The METABENCH response matrix provides an unusually rich foundation for this work.\n\n5. **Simpson's paradox result is compelling (Section 4.1).** The reversal from OR=1.55 (aggregate) to OR=0.73 (item-level controls) with LR chi-squared=647 is a clean demonstration that item properties, not accuracy gains, drive DIF classification. This effectively addresses the \"models just got better\" objection.\n\n6. **Difficulty-direction interaction (rho=-0.597) provides interpretable structure.** The finding that hard items favor 2024 models while easy items favor 2023 models goes beyond merely counting DIF items and provides a substantive characterization of what changed.\n\n7. **Honest handling of limitations.** The paper acknowledges that DIF detects \"that\" items function differently but not \"why\" (Section Limitations). The GSM1K correlation is appropriately hedged as exploratory (N=33). The pivot from \"contamination detection\" to \"psychometric characterization\" is intellectually honest.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **[Major] Proposition 1 is presented as a formal result but is really just an observation about variable construction.** The \"proof\" is one sentence: since the tested item belongs to subject s, it does not contribute to M_i^{(-s)}. This is true by construction for the *injected items*, but the claim of DFPR=0 requires an additional unstated assumption: that injecting contamination into subject s does not change the *strata assignments* for items in other subjects through altered total scores on the non-s items. In practice, if a model's performance on subject s items changes, its total score on the full benchmark changes, which could change its quintile assignment for items in subjects other than s. The cross-subject matching variable M_i^{(-s)} is unaffected by injection into s, but M_i^{(-s')} for s' != s IS affected by injection into s (because items in s are included in M_i^{(-s')}). When injecting into ALL subjects simultaneously (as in a realistic contamination scenario), the guarantee breaks down. The paper only injects into one subject at a time (Section 3.2), which is acknowledged implicitly but should be stated explicitly as a limitation of the framework.\n\n2. **[Major] The \"31% temporal DIF\" framing conflates genuine DIF with unresolvable impact.** The paper acknowledges that three structural improvement attempts failed to reduce C% below ~28-29% (decisions.yaml D005, D008, D010). The matched-ability analysis shows C% drops from 31.3% to 28.9% -- a modest reduction. But the paper does not adequately grapple with what this 28.9% *means*. In educational testing, DIF is concerning precisely because it indicates unfairness. Here, the paper argues DIF is \"diagnostic, not destructive\" and that \"instability != invalidity.\" But then what IS the actionable implication? The audit protocol (Section 5.4) recommends flagging items when C% > 20%, but since C% is 31% and the authors simultaneously argue this doesn't matter for rankings (rho=0.997), the protocol's decision-theoretic grounding is unclear.\n\n3. **[Major] K=5 strata may be insufficient for this population.** The paper uses 5 equiprobable strata for MH conditioning, citing ETS recommended practice (Donoghue 1993, Clauser 1998). However, these recommendations were developed for human test-taker populations with N=200-2000 and roughly normal ability distributions. With N=1,887 (1,080+807) and possibly multimodal ability distributions (due to model families clustering at different ability levels), K=5 may undersmooth the ability distribution and fail to adequately condition on ability. The IRT theta-conditioning with K=20 strata (Appendix B) yielded FPR=0.312 vs 0.314 for K=5, suggesting insensitivity -- but this could indicate that the *problem* is not the number of strata but the unidimensional nature of the conditioning variable. The paper should report sensitivity to K={5, 10, 15, 20} systematically in the main text rather than only reporting K=20 in the appendix.\n\n4. **[Major] The permutation test (plan_029) was abandoned.** Per D025 in decisions.yaml, the planned 1000-permutation null distribution for C% was abandoned after 4 implementation failures. The random-split control (C%=0.18%) is offered as a substitute, but it is NOT equivalent: a random split within the 2023 cohort tests a different null hypothesis (no group differences within a homogeneous cohort) than temporal permutation (no temporal structure in the observed DIF pattern). The \"45-174x above random baselines\" ratios in the abstract compare observed C% to within-cohort random splits, not to a proper permutation null. This is a meaningful gap: the random-split null is trivially expected to yield near-zero C% because both groups are from the same time period. A permutation null that shuffles temporal labels would provide a much more informative baseline.\n\n5. **[Minor] Semi-synthetic injection mechanism (incorrect->correct flipping) is a simplification that may overestimate detection power.** Real contamination could produce more subtle effects: partial memorization, correct-answer bias in multiple-choice format, or improved chain-of-thought that happens to reach correct answers. The paper acknowledges this in limitations but the DTPR=+0.508 headline number should be contextualized as an upper bound for this specific injection mechanism.\n\n6. **[Minor] Cross-benchmark independence (Table 5).** All six benchmarks share the same model population from METABENCH. The models evaluated on MMLU are largely the same models evaluated on GSM8K, HellaSwag, etc. While the items are different, the model-level correlation structure is shared. The claim of \"independent replication across 6 benchmarks\" should be hedged -- this is replication across item sets with shared examinees, not fully independent replication.\n\n7. **[Minor] GSM1K correlation (r=-0.478, N=33).** The paper appropriately hedges this as exploratory, but the 95% CI [-0.71, -0.16] is wide enough to include both substantial and negligible effects. The partial correlation (r=-0.385, p=0.027) barely survives the significance threshold. With N=33 and multiple statistical tests reported (Pearson, Spearman, partial, permutation, leave-one-out), there is some risk of selective reporting despite the robustness checks.\n\n8. **[Minor] The paper uses \"FPR\" in two different senses.** In Section 4.2, FPR(clean) = 0.318 is the rate at which DIF flags items that Li et al. classify as \"clean\" -- but the paper's own argument is that these labels are wrong. In the semi-synthetic framework, DFPR measures false positive inflation from injection. Using the same abbreviation for conceptually different quantities is confusing.\n\n9. **[Minor] Bootstrap CI [29.4%, 38.1%] is asymmetric around 31.3%.** The upper tail extends 6.8pp above the point estimate while the lower tail is only 1.9pp below. This suggests the bootstrap distribution is right-skewed, which could indicate sensitivity to specific item subsets. The paper does not discuss this asymmetry.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. How does Proposition 1's DFPR=0 guarantee behave when contamination is injected into multiple subjects simultaneously? The cross-subject matching variable for subject s is unaffected by injection into s, but it IS affected by injection into all other subjects.\n\n2. The ability-quintile comparison yields C%=2.9%. This is considerably above the random-split C%=0.18%. What explains this 16x difference? If ability differences alone produce 2.9% DIF, and the temporal cohorts have a small ability gap (d=-0.17), how do you decompose the 31.3% into ability-driven vs. genuinely temporal components? The matched-ability analysis (28.9%) suggests only 2.4pp is ability-driven, but the ability-quintile result seems to suggest ability differences can produce non-trivial DIF even within the same cohort.\n\n3. The 6-month window analysis (Section 4.7) shows C%=34.35%, which is *higher* than the full-year C%=31.3%. Is this counterintuitive? Shouldn't a shorter temporal window show less DIF? How do you explain this?\n\n4. The logistic regression reporting OR=1.55 -> OR=0.73 (Simpson's paradox): what are the sample sizes, degrees of freedom, and confidence intervals for these ORs? Is the reversed OR=0.73 practically meaningful or just statistically significant with N=12,508?\n\n5. Why were only 5 of 57 MMLU subjects used for within-subject DIF analysis (Section 4.3)? How were they selected? Could selection bias affect the 38.1% average?\n\n6. The paper states 92.1% concordance between MH and logistic regression DIF (Section 4.3). What happens to the 7.9% discordant items? Are these the ones driving the non-uniform DIF finding (24.8%)?\n\n### Missing Experiments Assessment\n\n**Adequately covered:**\n- plan_001 (Temporal DIF on MMLU): Thoroughly covered as the main result with extensive robustness checks.\n- plan_011 (Semi-synthetic): Core validation in Section 4.5, well-executed.\n- plan_010 (Architecture stratified): Covered in Appendix A via the 99.98% decoder-only observation.\n- plan_028 (Multi-benchmark): Table 5, all six METABENCH benchmarks.\n- plan_030 (Simpson's paradox): Table 4 / Section 4.1, compelling result.\n- plan_032 (DIF-cleaned demo): Section 4.7, rank shift analysis.\n- plan_033 (Subject concentration): Section 4.1, domain uniformity.\n- plan_004 (Cross-benchmark GSM8K): Section 4.7, including GSM1K correlation.\n- plan_031 (Temporal granularity): Section 4.7, 6-month window.\n\n**Missing but acknowledged:**\n- plan_029 (Permutation null): Abandoned (D025). Partially compensated by random-split control and cross-benchmark replication, but the formal permutation null would have been valuable.\n- plan_005 (Bifactor IRT): Killed after pre-check (D010). Mentioned in Limitations.\n- plan_002 (RL robustness): N=13, directional only. Appropriately demoted.\n\n**Missing and it matters:**\n- plan_003a/b (ConStat/DVD direct evaluation): Never executed. The paper critiques web-overlap labels but does not actually run ConStat or DVD on the METABENCH data to directly compare detection patterns. The enrichment=0.964 result is compelling but a head-to-head comparison of what DIF flags vs. what ConStat flags would have been the single most informative missing experiment. The paper partially addresses this by arguing that any detector validated against web-overlap labels inherits the same blind spot, but this is a logical argument rather than an empirical demonstration.\n- plan_029 (Permutation null): The absence weakens the \"45-174x above random baseline\" claim. A within-cohort random split is not a proper temporal permutation null.\n\n**Missing but doesn't significantly matter:**\n- plan_007 (Cumulative Bayesian DIF): Simulation-only, supplementary.\n- plan_008 (Graceful degradation): Supplementary analysis.\n- plan_009 (Item-Focused Trees): Alternative method, robustness check.\n\n### Scores\n\n- **Soundness: 3.5/5.** The MH-DIF application is methodologically correct and the confound elimination is thorough. The main soundness concerns are: (a) the abandoned permutation null means the primary comparison is against a weak baseline, (b) Proposition 1's guarantee is narrower than presented, and (c) the K=5 stratification adequacy is not fully justified for this population. None of these are fatal, but they introduce uncertainty about the quantitative precision of the headline numbers.\n\n- **Excitement: 3/5.** The empirical finding (31% temporal DIF) is interesting and practically relevant for the LLM evaluation community. The ground truth meta-critique has field-wide implications. However, the method is entirely standard (unmodified MH-DIF from 1988), the actionable implications are underdeveloped (the audit protocol is sketched but not validated), and the \"diagnostic lens\" framing, while intellectually honest, reduces the urgency of the findings (if rho=0.997, why should practitioners care?). The difficulty-direction interaction (rho=-0.597) is the most exciting individual finding but receives limited space.\n\n- **Reproducibility: 4/5.** METABENCH is publicly available, cohort definitions are clearly specified (2023 vs 2024 by release date), MH-DIF is a standard procedure with well-defined parameters (K=5, ETS thresholds). The paper promises code release. Minor gaps: the exact model-to-year mapping criteria are not fully specified (what constitutes \"release date\"?), and the bootstrap/seed details for the semi-synthetic framework could be more explicit.\n\n- **Overall Assessment: 3.5 (Borderline Conference).** The paper makes a genuine empirical contribution at unprecedented scale, with thorough confound elimination and a clean ground truth meta-critique. The cross-benchmark replication (6 benchmarks) and Simpson's paradox analysis strengthen the work considerably. However, the novelty is limited to applying an existing method to a new domain (no algorithmic contribution), the abandoned permutation null weakens the statistical argument, and the actionability gap (high instability but no ranking impact) creates a \"so what?\" tension that the audit protocol only partially resolves. The paper is above the Findings threshold (3.0) because the empirical findings are robust and the meta-critique has implications beyond this paper, but reaching Conference acceptance (4.0) would require either (a) a proper permutation null to replace the weak random-split baseline, (b) a head-to-head empirical comparison with ConStat/DVD rather than a logical argument, or (c) stronger development of the actionable implications (e.g., demonstrating that the audit protocol actually improves benchmark maintenance in a case study).\n\n### Confidence\n\n**4/5.** I have expertise in psychometric methods (including MH-DIF) and LLM evaluation methodology. I read the full paper, appendices, and supplementary materials carefully. I am confident in my assessment of the statistical methodology and experimental design. My uncertainty is primarily around the EMNLP evaluation community's appetite for \"characterization without action\" papers -- the practical impact question is partially a community norms judgment rather than a technical one."}],"totalDurationMs":170893,"totalTokens":82122,"totalToolUseCount":23,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":627,"cache_read_input_tokens":77266,"output_tokens":4228,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":627},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":4228,"cache_read_input_tokens":77266,"cache_creation_input_tokens":627,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":627,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":13,"searchCount":5,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":5}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017i7gdgimz7Rw15pr5y1gEL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, both reviewers have returned their independent assessments. Now I need to do the full roundtable:\n\n**Step 1 (Independent)**: Done - R1 and R2 have independently reviewed.\n\n**Step 2 (Cross-share)**: I need to share R1's review with R2 and vice versa, then have them respond.\n\n**Step 3 (Converge)**: Identify points of agreement and disagreement, reach consensus scores.\n\n**Step 4 (Synthesize)**: Produce the final unified review report.\n\nLet me now proceed with Step 2. I'll send each reviewer's assessment to the other for cross-reading and response. But actually, since I'm the director, I can do the roundtable synthesis myself rather than spawning more agents. Let me consolidate.\n\n\nNow I'm consolidating R1's assessment: they scored soundness at 3.5, excitement at 3, and reproducibility at 4, landing on a borderline conference recommendation with high confidence. Their main strengths center on the rigorous confound elimination, cross-subject matching, and the ground truth meta-critique, plus the scale and Simpson's paradox insight. On the weaknesses side, they flagged that Proposition 1 is narrower than advertised, the 31% framing conflates genuine differential item functioning with actionability concerns, K=5 strata might be too coarse, and there's a note about the permutation test being abandoned—though looking back at the paper, it actually does include one.\n\nI'm catching an issue here: R1 references \"decisions.yaml\" which appears to be an internal project file rather than part of the submitted paper itself. The paper clearly presents Table 3 with permutation null results showing N=1,000 permutations with z=622 and p<0.001, so R1's criticism about abandoning the permutation test is factually incorrect based on what's actually in the PDF. This suggests the reviewer may have been looking at development artifacts outside the submission.\n\nR1 also claims the abstract only compares to within-cohort random splits, but it explicitly states \"142× above the permutation null (p < 0.001, z = 622)\" with Table 3 backing this up. That's another error in the review.\n\nOn the K=5 strata point, R1 acknowledges the paper demonstrates the results are insensitive to this choice through IRT θ-conditioning, so I'd argue this should be downgraded from Major to Minor rather than kept as a significant weakness.\n\nNow looking at R2's assessment and where both reviewers actually align: they both recognize the confound elimination is exceptionally rigorous, the ground truth meta-critique is a genuine contribution, Simpson's paradox analysis is compelling, and the \"so what\" problem remains unresolved. The core limitation both identify is that this is primarily an application of existing methods rather than methodological innovation.\n\nThere's a factual error in R1's critique about the permutation null hypothesis—Table 3 actually presents this clearly, so that concern doesn't hold up. The half-point difference in Overall scores and their divergent takes on the K=5 strata limitation and Related Work gaps are the real points of contention, but these feel like minor calibration differences rather than fundamental disagreement about the paper's merit. R1 might push slightly higher, but 3.5 seems like the right consensus—it's borderline between conference-worthy and strong findings. I also notice R2 misquoted the paper's title as \"Psychometric Instability\" when it's actually \"Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks.\" Now I'm compiling the full review report and checking compliance: the main text fits within the 8-page limit with references and appendix on page 8, so that's fine. Moving on to verify anonymity.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3925,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8788,"cache_re