{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FcEXnqQ1neK5rWuf56ZAYb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S61xFo4bhCXQxY18Sy4czW","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report — dream_20260521T141728\n\n## Paper\n**Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks: A Large-Scale Differential Item Functioning Analysis**\n\n## Review Mode\nFull (2 Reviewers, 4-step roundtable)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThe paper applies Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items), finding 31.3% of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category C) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts—a rate 142× above the permutation null (z=622, p<0.001). The instability replicates across six benchmarks (17.9–31.3%), is not explained by capability improvement (≤2.4 pp; R²=0.0007), and is not driven by model non-independence or architecture heterogeneity. The paper critiques web-overlap labels as fundamentally mismatched for item-level differential detection (enrichment≈1.0), proposes a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000), and positions DIF as a diagnostic lens rather than a quality filter.\n\nFour claimed contributions: (1) temporal instability characterization, (2) ground truth meta-critique, (3) semi-synthetic validation framework, (4) DIF as diagnostic lens.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Reviewer Assignments\n\n- **R1** — Methods & Experiments (Soundness focus)\n- **R2** — Novelty & Related Work (Contribution focus)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Independent Reviews\n\n### R1: Methods & Experiments\n\n#### Strengths\n1. **Exceptionally thorough confound elimination (§4.3).** Six potential confounds systematically tested: architecture (99.98% decoder-only, Jaccard=1.0), ability gap (d=−0.17, matched C%=28.9%, 2.4pp confound), local independence (Q3=0.009), model non-independence (family-cap C%≤random at every K), IRT θ-conditioning (r=0.9986), non-uniform DIF (24.8% additional). The convergence of temporal matching (28.9%) and within-family Llama-2v3 matching (28.3%) at ~29% genuine DIF is particularly convincing.\n2. **Cross-subject matching achieves clean ΔFPR=0.000 (§3.3, Proposition 1).** The insight that within-subject matching absorbs injected contamination signal is subtle and practically important. Empirical confirmation across 5 subjects is well-executed.\n3. **Ground truth meta-critique is well-argued (§4.5).** Enrichment=0.964, Jaccard=0.206, φ=0.012 convincingly demonstrate orthogonality between DIF flags and web-overlap labels. The dose-response curve confirms DIF can detect differential contamination when present.\n4. **Scale is unprecedented.** 5,227 × 12,508 on MMLU, replicated across 6 benchmarks totaling ~27,000 items.\n5. **Simpson's paradox (Table 4) is compelling.** OR reversal from 1.55→0.73 (LR χ²=647) cleanly demonstrates item properties, not accuracy gains, drive DIF classification.\n6. **Difficulty×direction interaction (ρ=−0.597)** provides interpretable structure: hard items favor 2024, easy items favor 2023.\n7. **Honest handling of limitations.** GSM1K correlation hedged as exploratory (N=33). Bifactor failure disclosed. RL robustness acknowledged as underpowered.\n\n#### Weaknesses\n1. **[Major] Proposition 1 is narrower than presented.** The ΔFPR=0 guarantee holds when contamination is injected into a single subject s at a time. When contamination affects multiple subjects simultaneously (realistic scenario), the cross-subject matching variable for subject s IS affected by contamination in other subjects (since those items contribute to M_i^{(−s)}). The paper only tests single-subject injection. This limitation should be stated explicitly.\n2. **[Major] The \"31% temporal DIF\" framing leaves the actionability gap unresolved.** The matched-ability analysis shows ~29% genuine DIF. But the paper simultaneously argues ρ=0.997 (rankings barely change) and z=8.5 (systematic cohort bias). The audit protocol is a half-page sketch with vague thresholds (\"C%>20% triggers review\"). The decision-theoretic grounding—what should a benchmark maintainer actually *do* when 31% of items are flagged—is underdeveloped.\n3. **[Minor] Semi-synthetic injection (incorrect→correct flipping) is a simplification.** Real contamination could produce more subtle patterns (partial memorization, format-specific biases). ΔTPR=+0.508 should be contextualized as an upper bound for this injection mechanism. Acknowledged in Limitations but not sufficiently in the main text.\n4. **[Minor] Cross-benchmark \"replication\" shares the model population.** All six benchmarks use overlapping models from METABENCH. This is replication across item sets with shared examinees, not fully independent replication. The claim should be hedged accordingly.\n5. **[Minor] Bootstrap CI [29.4%, 38.1%] is asymmetric.** Upper tail extends 6.8pp above the point estimate vs 1.9pp below, suggesting right-skewed distribution and potential sensitivity to item subsets. Not discussed.\n6. **[Minor] \"FPR\" is used in two senses.** In §4.5, FPR(clean)=0.318 is the rate at which DIF flags items Li et al. classify as \"clean\" (but the paper argues these labels are wrong). In the semi-synthetic framework, ΔFPR measures false positive inflation from injection. Using the same abbreviation for different quantities is confusing.\n\n#### Questions for Authors\n1. How does Proposition 1 behave under simultaneous multi-subject contamination?\n2. The ability-quintile C%=2.9% is 16× above random-split C%=0.18%. What explains this gap if ability differences alone produce it?\n3. 6-month window C%=34.35% > full-year 31.3%. Why does a shorter window yield *more* DIF?\n4. Why were only 5 of 57 subjects used for within-subject DIF? How were they selected?\n\n#### R1 Scores\n- Soundness: 4/5\n- Excitement: 3/5\n- Reproducibility: 4/5\n- Overall: 3.5 (Borderline Conference)\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n### R2: Novelty & Related Work\n\n#### Strengths\n1. **Scale and thoroughness of controls** are substantially more rigorous than typical \"we applied method X and found result Y\" papers. The six-confound elimination with convergence at ~29% is the methodological backbone.\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique is genuinely insightful.** The distinction between global exposure and differential functioning identifies a systematic blind spot in the contamination detection literature's validation methodology. This insight extends beyond this paper.\n3. **Simpson's paradox finding is non-trivial.** The OR reversal (1.55→0.73) is not something one would expect a priori. Genuinely changes interpretation of the 31% headline number.\n4. **Cross-subject matching / Proposition 1** is practically valuable despite being conceptually straightforward. Clean ΔFPR=0.000 result.\n5. **\"Instability ≠ invalidity\" framing is mature.** The paper resists overclaiming. The circularity ablation (DIF is the *least* disruptive non-random removal strategy) demonstrates intellectual honesty.\n6. **Cross-benchmark replication** with interpretable task-type dichotomy (knowledge 27–31% > reasoning 18–19%) adds generalizability.\n\n#### Weaknesses\n1. **[Major] Core methodological contribution is primarily application, not innovation.** MH-DIF is a 1988 method. ETS classification is standard. Cross-subject matching has psychometric precedent (Nandakumar 1993, Donoghue & Allen 1993). Proposition 1 is mathematically trivial—removing a variable from the conditioning set eliminates its influence by definition. Labeling this a formal \"Proposition\" overstates the intellectual contribution.\n2. **[Major] The \"so what\" is not fully resolved.** If ρ=0.997, why should practitioners care? The audit protocol is a sketch, not a demonstrated improvement. The paper describes a phenomenon but does not show that acting on DIF findings improves benchmarks, evaluation, or models. The diagnostic lens framing is intellectually interesting but stops at description.\n3. **[Major] Causal ambiguity limits impact.** DIF detects *that* items function differently, not *why*. Is instability driven by contamination? Training methodology shifts? Data mixture composition? Without causal attribution, 31% is descriptive phenomenology. The GSM1K anti-correlation (r=−0.478, N=33) is correctly marked exploratory but provides only weak evidence for one pathway.\n4. **[Minor] Related Work has coverage gaps.** Missing engagement with: (a) benchmark saturation literature (Ott et al. 2022 in bib but not in text), (b) MMLU-Pro and other MMLU successors addressing item quality, (c) measurement invariance beyond DIF (multi-group CFA), (d) item parameter drift literature in educational measurement (Goldstein 1983, Wells et al. 2002), (e) IRT applications to NLP (Lalor et al. 2019, Rodriguez et al. 2021). Only 8 in-text references is thin for EMNLP.\n5. **[Minor] Proposition 1 assumes cross-subject ability is independent of injected contamination.** In a multidimensional ability space (57 MMLU subjects), contamination in subject X could affect the matching variable computed from subjects Y,Z if latent factor correlations are strong.\n6. **[Minor] Table 5 (cross-benchmark) lacks bootstrap CIs** for all benchmarks except MMLU. Unclear whether knowledge vs. reasoning C% differences are statistically significant.\n7. **[Minor] Discussion/Conclusion is compressed.** §5 Limitations + §6 Conclusion occupy ~0.7 pages total. The audit protocol, relationship to concurrent work, and implications for benchmark design all deserve more development.\n\n#### Novelty Verdict\nThe novelty profile is uneven. The core DIF analysis is textbook application. The ground truth meta-critique (§4.5) is the most novel intellectual contribution—identifying that the contamination detection literature's validation methodology is categorically mismatched is an insight the community needs. The Simpson's paradox and difficulty×direction interaction add genuine analytical novelty. Cross-subject matching is practically useful with honest acknowledgment of psychometric precedent. The combination of (a) unprecedented scale, (b) meta-critique, (c) Simpson's paradox, and (d) reusable validation framework constitutes a sufficient contribution, though closer to Findings than main conference.\n\n#### Related Work Gaps\n- IRT applications to NLP (Lalor et al. 2019, Rodriguez et al. 2021)\n- Benchmark dynamics / saturation (Kiela et al. 2021 Dynabench, Ott et al. 2022)\n- MMLU-Pro and MMLU successors (Wang et al. 2024)\n- Item parameter drift in educational measurement (Goldstein 1983, Wells et al. 2002)\n- Measurement invariance beyond DIF (Meredith 1993 / Vandenberg 2000 in bib but not discussed)\n- Contamination detection beyond Min-K%/ConStat/DVD (Golchin & Surdeanu 2023, Shi et al. 2024)\n\n#### R2 Scores\n- Soundness: 4/5\n- Excitement: 3/5\n- Reproducibility: 4/5\n- Overall: 3.0 (Findings)\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n## 4. Roundtable\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing Observations\n\n**R1 reading R2:** Agrees with R2's novelty concern—the method is standard, and the paper's value rests on empirical findings + meta-critique rather than algorithmic contribution. However, R1 notes that the *thoroughness* of confound elimination elevates this above a typical application paper. R1 agrees Related Work is thin (8 references in-text) and should engage with IRT-for-NLP literature.\n\n**R2 reading R1:** Accepts R1's concern about Proposition 1's scope (single-subject injection vs. multi-subject realistic scenario). R2 notes this aligns with their own Minor weakness #5. R2 agrees the actionability gap is the paper's central unresolved tension. R2 finds R1's FPR terminology concern valid and practically confusing.\n\n**Director correction:** R1's original draft flagged \"abandoned permutation null\" as a Major weakness based on an internal project file (decisions.yaml). This is factually incorrect—the submitted paper clearly presents Table 3 with 1,000 permutations (null mean=0.22%, null max=0.41%, z=622, p<0.001). This correction removes one of R1's Major weaknesses. R1's effective Soundness increases from the original 3.5 to 4/5, and the \"comparison to weak baseline\" concern is fully addressed by the permutation test.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\n\n**Points of agreement (both reviewers):**\n1. Confound elimination is the paper's methodological backbone—exceptionally thorough\n2. Ground truth meta-critique is the most novel intellectual contribution\n3. Simpson's paradox finding is genuine and non-trivial\n4. The \"so what\" / actionability is the central unresolved weakness\n5. Core novelty is limited to application of existing method + empirical findings\n6. Proposition 1 is correct but narrower than presented (single-subject injection)\n7. Reproducibility is strong (METABENCH public, MH-DIF standard, code promised)\n8. Related Work needs expansion (IRT-for-NLP, benchmark dynamics, measurement invariance)\n\n**Points of disagreement:**\n1. **Overall score:** R1 gives 3.5, R2 gives 3.0. The disagreement centers on how much weight to give the empirical thoroughness vs. methodological novelty. After discussion:\n   - R1 argues the six-confound elimination + cross-benchmark replication elevates this above typical Findings papers\n   - R2 argues that without actionable implications or methodological innovation, the bar for main conference (~21% accept rate) is not met\n   - **Consensus: 3.5 (Borderline Conference).** The combination of unprecedented scale, thorough confound elimination, ground truth meta-critique with field-wide implications, and the reusable semi-synthetic framework is above the Findings floor. The paper is on the border—the actionability gap is the key issue preventing a clear Conference recommendation.\n\n### Step 4: Consensus Assessment\n\n#### Consensus Scores\n| Dimension | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| Soundness | 4/5 | Methodology is correct, confound elimination thorough, permutation null airtight (z=622). Proposition 1 has a scope limitation (single-subject) but empirical results are clean. Minor issues with FPR terminology and bootstrap CI asymmetry. |\n| Excitement | 3/5 | Ground truth meta-critique and Simpson's paradox are genuinely exciting. Difficulty×direction interaction provides mechanistic insight. But core method is standard, actionable implications underdeveloped, and \"instability ≠ invalidity\" narrative partially undermines urgency. |\n| Reproducibility | 4/5 | METABENCH public, MH-DIF standard procedure, cohort definitions clear, code promised. Minor gaps in exact implementation details. |\n| **Overall** | **3.5** | **Borderline Conference** |\n\n#### Justification for 3.5\nThe paper is above the Findings threshold (3.0) for three reasons: (1) the ground truth meta-critique identifies a systematic blind spot in the contamination detection literature's validation methodology—this has implications beyond this paper; (2) the six-confound elimination sets a new standard for rigor in benchmark analysis papers; (3) the cross-benchmark replication (6 benchmarks, 17.9–31.3%, all >45× enrichment) provides decisive evidence that temporal DIF is a general benchmark phenomenon.\n\nThe paper falls short of a clear Conference recommendation (4.0) for three reasons: (1) the core method is unmodified MH-DIF from 1988—no algorithmic novelty; (2) the actionability gap—31% instability with ρ=0.997 rankings creates a \"so what?\" tension the audit protocol only sketches; (3) causal ambiguity—DIF detects *that* items function differently but not *why*, limiting the diagnostic value.\n\n**What would move this to 4.0:**\n- A demonstrated case study showing the audit protocol improves benchmark maintenance in practice\n- Head-to-head empirical comparison with ConStat/DVD (not just logical argument from orthogonality)\n- Deeper development of the difficulty×direction interaction into a predictive model of which items will become unstable\n- Addressing Proposition 1's multi-subject injection limitation with additional experiments\n\n---\n\n## 5. Key Strengths (Consensus)\n\n1. **Unprecedented scale + thorough controls.** 5,227 models × 12,508 items, 6 benchmarks, 6 confounds eliminated. The convergence of temporal matching (28.9%) and within-family matching (28.3%) at ~29% genuine DIF is the paper's most convincing single result.\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique (§4.5).** The argument that web-overlap labels are categorically wrong for evaluating differential detectors is the paper's most novel contribution, with implications for the entire contamination detection literature.\n3. **Simpson's paradox (Table 4).** OR reversal from 1.55→0.73 is a non-trivial analytical finding that substantively changes the interpretation of the 31% headline.\n4. **Cross-subject matching with ΔFPR=0.000.** Practically useful for anyone doing semi-synthetic DIF validation on multi-domain benchmarks.\n5. **Mature scientific framing.** \"Instability ≠ invalidity\" resists overclaiming. Honest disclosure of killed experiments (bifactor IRT), underpowered analyses (RL robustness), and exploratory results (GSM1K correlation).\n\n## 6. Key Weaknesses (Consensus)\n\n1. **[Major] Actionability gap.** The paper's central tension—31% instability with ρ=0.997—is identified but not resolved. The audit protocol is a sketch. What should a practitioner *do* with DIF flags?\n2. **[Major] Limited methodological novelty.** MH-DIF is standard. Cross-subject matching has precedent. Proposition 1 is correct but mathematically trivial and limited to single-subject injection. The paper's value is empirical, not algorithmic.\n3. **[Major] Causal ambiguity.** DIF flags *that* items function differently but not *why*. Without causal attribution (contamination? capability shift? training data composition?), the 31% number is descriptive phenomenology.\n4. **[Minor] Related Work is thin.** 8 in-text references. Missing engagement with IRT-for-NLP, benchmark dynamics, measurement invariance, item parameter drift literature.\n5. **[Minor] Proposition 1 scope limitation.** ΔFPR=0 holds for single-subject injection but not necessarily for simultaneous multi-subject contamination. Should be stated explicitly.\n6. **[Minor] Cross-benchmark replication shares model population.** Not fully independent replication; should be hedged.\n\n## 7. Questions for Authors (Priority-Ordered)\n\n1. How does Proposition 1's ΔFPR=0 guarantee behave under simultaneous multi-subject contamination?\n2. What should a benchmark maintainer concretely *do* when 31% of items are flagged? Can you demonstrate the audit protocol on even one benchmark version update?\n3. The 6-month window C%=34.35% exceeds the full-year C%=31.3%. Why does a shorter window yield more DIF? What does this imply about the temporal dynamics of item instability?\n4. Can you distinguish \"DIF captures differential contamination that web-overlap misses\" from \"DIF captures something unrelated to contamination entirely (e.g., item ambiguity manifesting differently across generations)\"?\n5. Why was MH-DIF chosen over logistic regression DIF as the primary method, given that LR-DIF captures both uniform and non-uniform DIF and your headline number would be substantially higher?\n\n## 8. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\n| Experiment | Status | Assessment |\n|------------|--------|------------|\n| plan_001: Temporal DIF MMLU | ✓ Main result | Thorough with full robustness |\n| plan_002: RL robustness | ✓ Appendix A | N=13, directional. Appropriately hedged |\n| plan_003a/b: ConStat/DVD eval | ✗ Not executed | Cited from literature. Missing head-to-head is a gap |\n| plan_003c: Ensemble fusion | ✗ Dropped | Clean scope reduction with pivot to characterization |\n| plan_004: Cross-benchmark GSM8K | ✓ Table 6 | Including semi-synthetic + GSM1K correlation |\n| plan_005: Bifactor IRT | ✗ Killed | Disclosed in Limitations. Informative negative |\n| plan_006: Item generation | ✗ Blocked | Cascade from plan_005. Out of scope |\n| plan_007: Cumulative Bayesian | ✓ Appendix A | Simulation only. Nice supplementary |\n| plan_008: Graceful degradation | ✓ §4.7 | Progressive removal analysis |\n| plan_009: Item-Focused Trees | ✓ Appendix A | Robustness check, Jaccard=0.78 with MH |\n| plan_010: Architecture stratified | ✓ §4.3 | 99.98% decoder-only, clean elimination |\n| plan_011: Semi-synthetic | ✓ §4.6, Table 7 | Core validation, well-executed |\n| plan_012: IRT θ-conditioning | ✓ §4.3 | r=0.9986, identical results |\n| plan_013: Q3 local independence | ✓ §4.3 | Mean Q3=0.009, negligible |\n| plan_014: Within-subject DIF | ✓ §4.3 (implicit) | C%=38.1%, confirms cross-subject doesn't inflate |\n| plan_017: Downstream impact | ✓ §4.7 | ρ=0.9965, z=−14.91 |\n| plan_018: Threshold sensitivity | ✓ §4.2 | 10–49% range, BH removes 3/3918 |\n| plan_019: Item-level decomposition | ✓ Table 4 | R²=0.0007, Simpson's paradox |\n| plan_020: Circularity ablation | ✓ §4.7 | DIF least disruptive non-random strategy |\n| plan_021: LR non-uniform DIF | ✓ §4.3 | 92.1% concordance, 24.8% additional |\n| plan_022: Model independence | ✓ §4.3 | Graduated de-duplication at every K |\n| plan_023: Item characterization | ✓ §4.7 | d=−0.247, ρ=−0.597 |\n| plan_026: Direct Li comparison | ✓ §4.5 | enrichment=0.964, φ=0.012 |\n| plan_027: Temporal ability overlap | ✓ §4.3 | d=−0.17, matched C%=28.9% |\n| plan_028: Multi-benchmark | ✓ Table 5 | 6 benchmarks, 17.9–31.3% |\n| plan_029: Permutation null | ✓ Table 3 | z=622, p<0.001 |\n| plan_030: Simpson's paradox | ✓ Table 4 | OR reversal 1.55→0.73 |\n| plan_031: Temporal granularity | ✓ §4.4 | 6-month C%=34.35% |\n| plan_032: DIF-cleaned demo | ✓ §4.7 | z=8.5 cohort asymmetry |\n| plan_033: Subject concentration | ✓ §4.1 | All 57 subjects, KW p=0.78 |\n\n**Coverage: 27/31 experiments executed or appropriately killed/blocked. 4 not executed (3a, 3b, 3c, 6), all acknowledged.** Experiment coverage is very high—virtually all executable experiments are reflected in the paper.\n\n## 9. Format Compliance (EMNLP 2026)\n\n- **Page limit:** 8 pages main text (Sections 1–6). References unlimited. Appendix A on page 8. ✓ Compliant.\n- **Anonymity:** \"Anonymous\" author. No obvious deanonymization signals. ✓\n- **Limitations section:** Present (§5, ~1/3 page, 3 limitations). ✓\n- **References:** 8 cited works. Thin but not a desk-reject signal. All appear real.\n- **Responsible NLP Checklist:** Not visible in PDF. ⚠️ Must be included for ARR submission.\n\n## 10. Final Verdict\n\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Soundness | 4/5 |\n| Excitement | 3/5 |\n| Reproducibility | 4/5 |\n| **Overall Assessment** | **3.5 — Borderline Conference** |\n| Reviewer Confidence | 4/5 (both reviewers) |\n\n**Bottom line:** A technically sound, empirically thorough characterization paper at unprecedented scale. The ground truth meta-critique and Simpson's paradox are genuine contributions. The confound elimination sets a new rigor standard. However, the core method is standard (no algorithmic novelty), the actionability gap is unresolved, and causal ambiguity limits the diagnostic value. Borderline between strong Findings and Conference acceptance—the meta-critique's field-wide implications tip toward Conference, but the \"so what\" gap holds it back.\n","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260521T141728","overall_score":"3.5 — Borderline Conference","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":713,"cache_read_input_tokens":85209,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":713},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e51e0195-9c84-4e56-b272-a71ad93d54d2","uuid":"e6f10d49-82b4-4358-a4d1-2b525df638e7"}
